1
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:50,360
A face... a fleet... a wall... a

2
00:00:58,450 --> 00:00:59,450
man...

3
00:00:59,450 --> 00:01:04,870
A trick... a trick to break the walls of
Troy.

4
00:01:07,570 --> 00:01:08,710
And burn it.

5
00:01:09,090 --> 00:01:10,130
Scream into the ground.

6
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:14,150
What does

7
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:02,900
it mean?

8
00:02:12,790 --> 00:02:17,160
A gift... for Athena.

9
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:21,420
Common gods.

10
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:23,900
Respect their offering.

11
00:02:28,030 --> 00:02:29,530
Save it from the waves.

12
00:02:30,050 --> 00:02:31,250
Take it to Athena's temple.

13
00:02:32,290 --> 00:02:33,390
The war is over.

14
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:02,060
Not this song.

15
00:03:14,510 --> 00:03:16,270
Remind them of who's wine they're
drinking.

16
00:03:16,690 --> 00:03:18,190
I don't want to deceive this song.

17
00:03:18,710 --> 00:03:19,650
I want to deceive us.

18
00:03:19,990 --> 00:03:22,650
Why do you entertain shooters in his house
night after night?

19
00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:26,870
In this world, a man does what he chooses.

20
00:03:27,710 --> 00:03:28,590
I do what I can.

21
00:03:28,590 --> 00:03:30,050
I am a man.

22
00:03:30,330 --> 00:03:30,870
Now I'm all.

23
00:03:32,030 --> 00:03:33,050
So throw them out.

24
00:03:34,230 --> 00:03:37,230
Break Zeus' law and see what they do to
you if you give them that excuse.

25
00:03:43,350 --> 00:03:45,130
I've already lost a husband.

26
00:03:46,170 --> 00:03:47,150
I don't want to lose a son.

27
00:03:47,870 --> 00:03:48,910
My dad's coming back.

28
00:03:49,210 --> 00:03:50,030
And where is he?

29
00:03:50,930 --> 00:03:53,150
Eight years since Troy fell.

30
00:03:53,790 --> 00:03:55,430
Almost twenty since he left us.

31
00:03:55,670 --> 00:03:56,290
Where is he?

32
00:03:58,870 --> 00:03:59,350
We

33
00:04:09,550 --> 00:04:10,470
welcomed all of you.

34
00:04:10,470 --> 00:04:11,910
Are you calling us beggars?

35
00:04:12,110 --> 00:04:14,390
I'm saying you've been welcome here for
three years.

36
00:04:14,930 --> 00:04:17,450
We've only enjoyed your hospitality for so
long.

37
00:04:19,330 --> 00:04:20,650
Because your mother won't choose.

38
00:04:21,610 --> 00:04:22,790
She's already married.

39
00:04:23,230 --> 00:04:23,990
Odysseus is dead.

40
00:04:24,790 --> 00:04:26,230
What do you know about my dad?

41
00:04:29,190 --> 00:04:31,690
I know he kept stringing cattle in the bad
wine.

42
00:04:32,550 --> 00:04:34,530
You had the best of it the first two
seasons.

43
00:04:35,010 --> 00:04:36,350
I know he never came home.

44
00:04:37,050 --> 00:04:37,550
Not yet.

45
00:04:39,250 --> 00:04:42,250
You're pining for a daddy you didn't even
know.

46
00:04:43,210 --> 00:04:44,930
Like some sniffling bastard.

47
00:04:45,810 --> 00:04:48,050
Well, since none of us can really know who
our fathers are.

48
00:04:48,410 --> 00:04:49,190
Don't cling to no one.

49
00:04:50,050 --> 00:04:51,010
But I did know him.

50
00:04:51,690 --> 00:04:52,190
As boys.

51
00:04:52,830 --> 00:04:53,590
We watched him hunt.

52
00:04:55,650 --> 00:04:58,190
I tried to volunteer for his expedition to
Troy.

53
00:04:58,530 --> 00:05:00,950
He told me to stay here and keep an eye on
you.

54
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:02,510
Oh, me or my mother?

55
00:05:04,370 --> 00:05:05,710
You can't blame me for looking.

56
00:05:05,710 --> 00:05:07,410
My dad is coming home.

57
00:05:07,950 --> 00:05:10,130
And his hospitality won't be as generous
as hers.

58
00:05:10,650 --> 00:05:12,130
So he doesn't follow Zeus' law then?

59
00:05:12,730 --> 00:05:16,750
Zeus' law tells us to treat strangers as
we be treated because they might be gods

60
00:05:16,750 --> 00:05:17,310
in disguise.

61
00:05:18,450 --> 00:05:20,870
I think we've established to antennae us
that you were not a god.

62
00:05:22,430 --> 00:05:24,130
This one certainly doesn't smell like a
god.

63
00:05:24,390 --> 00:05:26,030
Wouldn't be much of a disguise if he did.

64
00:05:26,390 --> 00:05:26,710
Careful.

65
00:05:27,830 --> 00:05:29,850
He might strike you down right now.

66
00:05:36,030 --> 00:05:36,510
Connor.

67
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:45,440
That thunder was luck.

68
00:05:46,660 --> 00:05:47,240
I don't know.

69
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:49,140
You have wise eyes.

70
00:05:50,780 --> 00:05:51,340
Themis eyes.

71
00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:53,100
Here, you see all the traitors?

72
00:05:54,420 --> 00:05:55,140
From Sparta.

73
00:05:55,760 --> 00:05:57,040
Has Menelaus come home from the war?

74
00:05:58,480 --> 00:05:59,240
Years ago.

75
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:00,880
Oh, did Zeus never came back?

76
00:06:01,460 --> 00:06:02,840
We don't even know if he's alive.

77
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:03,500
Have you heard anything?

78
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:05,540
The song of how he won the war.

79
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:06,720
We've all heard that.

80
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:07,320
Anything else?

81
00:06:08,020 --> 00:06:08,280
No.

82
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:10,960
But...

83
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:13,800
Sparta is full of Trojan War veterans.

84
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:15,300
Someone will know something.

85
00:06:16,360 --> 00:06:17,600
You will this is his son?

86
00:06:18,380 --> 00:06:19,240
Go to Sparta.

87
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:20,680
Go.

88
00:06:21,240 --> 00:06:22,240
See Menelaus.

89
00:06:23,340 --> 00:06:24,120
He'd welcome you.

90
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:27,800
The queen wants to speak to the stranger.

91
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:30,860
She lifts her nose.

92
00:06:34,220 --> 00:06:36,020
Just don't try to give her any false hope.

93
00:06:36,020 --> 00:06:39,180
She has some way of knowing whether a
person has actually seen Odysseus.

94
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:40,500
You see that, man?

95
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:44,240
He can't see much anymore, but that
doesn't stop him beating travelers to

96
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:46,920
death in exchange for lies about my
father.

97
00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:15,880
No, no, too fast.

98
00:07:16,900 --> 00:07:17,740
How can you know?

99
00:07:18,120 --> 00:07:20,800
You don't need eyes to hear a hasty
combination.

100
00:07:21,760 --> 00:07:22,940
Mentor push him.

101
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:31,540
No.

102
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:32,180
No, too fast.

103
00:07:41,580 --> 00:07:42,980
He's as friendly as you.

104
00:07:44,180 --> 00:07:45,660
He only answered to your father.

105
00:07:46,380 --> 00:07:47,460
Where'd he get him from?

106
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:49,460
He met right here.

107
00:07:54,220 --> 00:07:55,340
Come on.

108
00:07:57,320 --> 00:07:57,760
Ah.

109
00:07:59,100 --> 00:07:59,860
I'll take him.

110
00:08:00,540 --> 00:08:01,120
For your boy?

111
00:08:01,780 --> 00:08:02,540
We're a hunting dog.

112
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:06,160
Damned if he wasn't right.

113
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:42,340
Hold him still.

114
00:08:42,880 --> 00:08:43,960
How bad a scar do you want?

115
00:08:45,780 --> 00:08:47,300
Why do you always pluck your bow?

116
00:08:47,980 --> 00:08:48,660
It's only fair.

117
00:08:49,220 --> 00:08:50,040
To warn your prey?

118
00:08:50,880 --> 00:08:51,800
To fight with honor.

119
00:08:52,120 --> 00:08:53,240
You weren't fighting, you were hunting.

120
00:08:54,020 --> 00:08:54,840
Hunting's nothing.

121
00:08:55,200 --> 00:08:55,900
Fighting with stealth.

122
00:08:56,540 --> 00:08:56,940
Clever.

123
00:08:57,600 --> 00:08:59,760
Clever, but your cleverness is going to
get you in trouble.

124
00:09:00,620 --> 00:09:01,380
Try again.

125
00:09:02,200 --> 00:09:04,420
This time, not as fast or precise.

126
00:09:04,620 --> 00:09:06,780
You can't win with a perfect defense.

127
00:09:07,260 --> 00:09:08,060
Draw the attack.

128
00:09:08,360 --> 00:09:08,700
How?

129
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:09,940
Hesitate.

130
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:13,000
Open your belly to your opponent's blade.

131
00:09:13,200 --> 00:09:14,940
Their attack becomes your chance.

132
00:09:46,290 --> 00:09:47,550
You teach my father that?

133
00:09:48,210 --> 00:09:49,670
No, no, he taught me.

134
00:09:50,690 --> 00:09:56,150
He said, you're not always going to be the
strongest or the fastest, so be the

135
00:09:56,150 --> 00:09:56,570
smartest.

136
00:09:57,590 --> 00:09:58,630
What would he do, huh?

137
00:09:59,590 --> 00:10:01,870
If he came back here to find all these
suitors in his house?

138
00:10:02,130 --> 00:10:02,430
Don't.

139
00:10:02,570 --> 00:10:05,230
I can't even let myself imagine what he'd
do to them.

140
00:10:05,590 --> 00:10:06,310
There are so many.

141
00:10:07,210 --> 00:10:09,010
Cut a couple down, the others cave.

142
00:10:09,310 --> 00:10:10,070
Bullies and bullies.

143
00:10:10,070 --> 00:10:10,630
Cowards.

144
00:10:11,450 --> 00:10:14,970
Most of them are old enough to go to Troy,
but when they saw Agamemnon's sail,

145
00:10:18,350 --> 00:10:19,790
their fathers hit them.

146
00:10:23,490 --> 00:10:27,250
And you knew Agamemnon's war would take
many years and many lives.

147
00:10:32,570 --> 00:10:34,490
So he raised his army by lottery.

148
00:10:37,510 --> 00:10:37,990
Name?

149
00:10:38,370 --> 00:10:38,710
Draw.

150
00:10:39,770 --> 00:10:40,010
No.

151
00:10:40,630 --> 00:10:41,070
Name?

152
00:10:41,430 --> 00:10:41,870
Antinous.

153
00:10:42,110 --> 00:10:42,350
Draw.

154
00:10:44,830 --> 00:10:45,270
Yes.

155
00:10:45,830 --> 00:10:46,310
By lots?

156
00:10:49,630 --> 00:10:51,090
Antinous told me he volunteered.

157
00:11:01,580 --> 00:11:03,760
Your boy's trying to take Sinon's place.

158
00:11:04,780 --> 00:11:05,560
I'm not going to let him.

159
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:07,940
He needs to stay here, take care of his
family.

160
00:11:10,820 --> 00:11:13,080
You can watch over your boy, too,
of course.

161
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:18,400
He's with me.

162
00:11:19,320 --> 00:11:21,560
He wanted Antinous to look after me.

163
00:11:21,700 --> 00:11:22,300
No, no, no.

164
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:25,460
But Odysseus was sensitive to a father's
shame.

165
00:11:26,080 --> 00:11:26,920
He felt for these fathers.

166
00:11:27,740 --> 00:11:30,920
You and your mother meant more to him than
promises of glory.

167
00:11:32,040 --> 00:11:33,660
So he knew it would be years.

168
00:11:34,900 --> 00:11:36,040
Did he tell my mother?

169
00:11:36,680 --> 00:11:38,020
That's a question for her.

170
00:11:38,420 --> 00:11:38,780
You miss.

171
00:11:39,660 --> 00:11:41,060
Is he ever coming back?

172
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:44,000
Hargis thinks so.

173
00:11:44,540 --> 00:11:46,140
Never known a dog reach 20.

174
00:11:47,380 --> 00:11:49,340
But he's long not lived his master.

175
00:12:06,070 --> 00:12:07,750
I didn't mean to stop you.

176
00:12:10,710 --> 00:12:11,910
I'm in no hurry.

177
00:12:13,310 --> 00:12:17,650
The elder's decreed when I finish your
grandfather's burial shroud, it'll be time

178
00:12:17,650 --> 00:12:18,150
to remarry.

179
00:12:18,730 --> 00:12:19,110
Why?

180
00:12:24,870 --> 00:12:28,730
If it has been without a king too long,
they want security.

181
00:12:29,410 --> 00:12:30,070
From what?

182
00:12:30,570 --> 00:12:31,470
The people from the sea.

183
00:12:32,450 --> 00:12:33,790
That's just travelers' tales.

184
00:12:34,690 --> 00:12:36,750
All we know of the world is travelers'
tales.

185
00:12:36,870 --> 00:12:37,810
That's how we found Dr. I.

186
00:12:37,890 --> 00:12:38,310
and Paulin.

187
00:12:39,230 --> 00:12:42,170
Now they all tell stories of attacks by
people from the sea.

188
00:12:43,530 --> 00:12:45,190
And who are the people from the sea?

189
00:12:45,790 --> 00:12:48,430
No one knows, but the elders wanted they
could be prepared.

190
00:12:48,990 --> 00:12:49,430
Prepared?

191
00:12:50,030 --> 00:12:52,250
Part of the greatest civilization ever
known.

192
00:12:54,230 --> 00:12:55,410
Look at this great house.

193
00:12:56,230 --> 00:12:57,530
Stone's as old as knowledge.

194
00:12:58,350 --> 00:13:01,110
At the mercy of some greedy, unwanted
guests.

195
00:13:02,290 --> 00:13:03,510
The structure's nothing.

196
00:13:04,710 --> 00:13:06,670
Without people's respect for its meaning.

197
00:13:07,170 --> 00:13:08,010
It was Zeus's law.

198
00:13:09,090 --> 00:13:10,630
To treat others as he would be treated.

199
00:13:11,570 --> 00:13:13,550
And they abuse that, they destroy
everything.

200
00:13:14,770 --> 00:13:16,810
So how could you think of marrying one of
them?

201
00:13:17,170 --> 00:13:22,150
Because a long time ago, your father asked
me to.

202
00:13:23,230 --> 00:13:24,450
Agamemnon is our king.

203
00:13:24,450 --> 00:13:27,070
And the Trojans took his brother's wife.

204
00:13:27,230 --> 00:13:28,590
Well, she ran away with them.

205
00:13:29,250 --> 00:13:32,510
Either way, the walls of Troy are said to
be impregnable.

206
00:13:33,690 --> 00:13:37,850
We'll sit outside that great city trying
to draw them into battle.

207
00:13:40,430 --> 00:13:45,670
As the years go by, and Telemachus grows,
and you learn life without me.

208
00:13:46,530 --> 00:13:48,190
Menelaus will give up on Helen.

209
00:13:53,070 --> 00:13:57,990
This is Agamemnon's excuse to break Troy's
control of the trading routes.

210
00:13:59,970 --> 00:14:01,650
He won't let it pass.

211
00:14:02,910 --> 00:14:03,210
Ever.

212
00:14:10,220 --> 00:14:12,020
Who have you refused?

213
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:19,580
He'll take our son.

214
00:14:21,600 --> 00:14:22,520
He would never.

215
00:14:23,600 --> 00:14:26,760
He sacrificed his own daughter for
favorable winds.

216
00:14:28,340 --> 00:14:30,300
Well, the gods want his daughter's life.

217
00:14:30,300 --> 00:14:38,040
The power of a sacrifice is in the cost to
the person making it.

218
00:14:40,380 --> 00:14:41,860
It's monstrous.

219
00:14:44,600 --> 00:14:45,860
And committed.

220
00:14:49,390 --> 00:14:50,860
I won't return soon.

221
00:14:51,800 --> 00:14:52,920
If at all.

222
00:14:57,410 --> 00:14:58,810
What have we ran?

223
00:15:00,810 --> 00:15:06,230
Took you past the ship, the brightest
crew, and ahead for the horizon.

224
00:15:08,030 --> 00:15:14,450
Held hands on deck as we rode west at
dusk, chasing the escaping sun.

225
00:15:18,050 --> 00:15:19,810
There's a world beyond these walls.

226
00:15:21,530 --> 00:15:26,390
Agamemnon's vengeance would fall on all of
it, the god.

227
00:15:32,630 --> 00:15:40,270
And I promise me, if I don't return,
by the time Telemachus comes of age,

228
00:15:40,870 --> 00:15:45,250
promise me, you'll take another husband.

229
00:15:47,790 --> 00:15:49,030
Enough sacrifice.

230
00:15:49,490 --> 00:15:50,950
Love's not a sacrifice.

231
00:15:51,350 --> 00:15:51,970
Of course it is.

232
00:15:53,510 --> 00:15:55,050
I don't want you to make it.

233
00:15:55,150 --> 00:15:56,050
It's not up to you.

234
00:15:58,890 --> 00:15:59,790
Promise me.

235
00:16:01,730 --> 00:16:02,050
No.

236
00:16:06,300 --> 00:16:07,920
Promise me you'll come back.

237
00:16:13,560 --> 00:16:14,920
What if I can't?

238
00:16:20,780 --> 00:16:23,340
Then your memory won't be making my
decisions.

239
00:16:28,540 --> 00:16:29,560
Take my pen.

240
00:16:30,740 --> 00:16:31,380
Wear it.

241
00:16:31,600 --> 00:16:32,040
Always.

242
00:16:32,620 --> 00:16:33,880
Athena will bring you home.

243
00:16:34,240 --> 00:16:35,800
I'm not sure it suits me.

244
00:16:38,680 --> 00:16:40,760
My drablers claim to have seen you.

245
00:16:41,420 --> 00:16:44,440
I'll ask where you were wearing,
but I'll know if they're lying.

246
00:16:48,260 --> 00:16:54,600
If the people from the sea are real,
right now, Ithaca has no king or as an

247
00:16:54,600 --> 00:16:54,820
army.

248
00:16:55,700 --> 00:16:57,000
I could take this room.

249
00:16:59,480 --> 00:16:59,960
Come.

250
00:17:12,740 --> 00:17:13,420
String it.

251
00:17:23,860 --> 00:17:24,780
That's not fair.

252
00:17:25,780 --> 00:17:27,360
You said only he could string this bow.

253
00:17:27,840 --> 00:17:29,220
He was happy to let others try.

254
00:17:29,220 --> 00:17:29,280
Really?

255
00:17:31,980 --> 00:17:33,560
He'd line up all the axes.

256
00:18:05,780 --> 00:18:07,320
No one else ever did it.

257
00:18:08,960 --> 00:18:12,740
Mom, they're bleeding us dry, so you'll be
forced to choose one of them before I can

258
00:18:12,740 --> 00:18:13,840
claim the throne myself.

259
00:18:14,800 --> 00:18:17,680
The traveler told me Menelaus returned
years ago.

260
00:18:18,040 --> 00:18:18,800
They've all returned.

261
00:18:18,800 --> 00:18:19,780
All who survived.

262
00:18:21,940 --> 00:18:22,940
Maybe he is dead.

263
00:18:23,880 --> 00:18:25,640
And if he is, then this is my palace.

264
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:28,020
We need to know.

265
00:18:28,500 --> 00:18:30,540
I have to go to Menelaus to find out the
truth.

266
00:18:31,080 --> 00:18:34,560
Do that because these suitors mean you
harm.

267
00:18:34,840 --> 00:18:35,960
Oh, you think I don't know that?

268
00:18:37,100 --> 00:18:39,820
Every night they push me to give them the
excuse they need.

269
00:18:39,860 --> 00:18:44,700
And out there on the sea, on the road,
they won't need an excuse.

270
00:18:44,940 --> 00:18:45,760
You just won't come back.

271
00:18:47,800 --> 00:18:49,880
You don't want me to claim the throne
myself.

272
00:18:51,960 --> 00:18:56,020
You just want to keep me in my place so
that you can stay Queen of Ithaca.

273
00:18:56,060 --> 00:18:58,860
I've had enough of being Queen of Ithaca
without my king!

274
00:18:59,240 --> 00:19:00,180
Without your king?

275
00:19:01,780 --> 00:19:02,840
Or a king?

276
00:19:04,260 --> 00:19:06,900
And you think you're ready to take your
father's place?

277
00:19:09,060 --> 00:19:10,000
Your child.

278
00:19:19,340 --> 00:19:22,720
Out in the land he is.

279
00:19:23,060 --> 00:19:24,800
Don't wear your spurs in the palace.

280
00:19:25,020 --> 00:19:26,980
You're a cowherd, not a horseman.

281
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:30,080
And you're a pig farmer, and who are you
to talk to me like that?

282
00:19:30,780 --> 00:19:33,980
A servant our master left in charge of his
herdsmen.

283
00:19:34,700 --> 00:19:36,660
The master is long gone.

284
00:19:36,860 --> 00:19:37,700
You're blind or full.

285
00:19:38,480 --> 00:19:41,320
And you're about as welcome here as that
doggess.

286
00:19:42,260 --> 00:19:42,680
Argus!

287
00:19:46,680 --> 00:19:47,200
Argus!

288
00:19:49,200 --> 00:19:49,720
Argus!

289
00:19:49,720 --> 00:19:49,840
Argus!

290
00:19:53,080 --> 00:19:53,480
Sorry.

291
00:19:54,220 --> 00:19:55,120
He didn't do anything.

292
00:19:55,260 --> 00:19:56,180
So that stink is you?

293
00:19:57,440 --> 00:19:58,720
He be old dog alone.

294
00:19:59,380 --> 00:19:59,900
Which one?

295
00:20:00,120 --> 00:20:00,840
Both of us.

296
00:20:02,180 --> 00:20:03,420
What did you say to him, slave?

297
00:20:03,480 --> 00:20:04,340
He just got carried away.

298
00:20:04,520 --> 00:20:06,120
Argus is my father's hunting dog.

299
00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:07,160
It's hunting dog.

300
00:20:11,400 --> 00:20:12,380
Look at that thing.

301
00:20:14,120 --> 00:20:14,900
Get it out of here.

302
00:20:15,020 --> 00:20:16,920
Don't catch his ears in pain.

303
00:20:17,680 --> 00:20:18,080
Okay.

304
00:20:18,880 --> 00:20:20,220
You're not put it out of his misery.

305
00:20:20,600 --> 00:20:21,620
Leave him alone!

306
00:20:41,130 --> 00:20:42,190
You all see?

307
00:20:51,340 --> 00:20:52,720
Please don't hurt my son.

308
00:20:56,120 --> 00:20:56,960
Easy, easy.

309
00:20:57,300 --> 00:20:57,620
All of us.

310
00:20:58,580 --> 00:20:59,700
Young pup lost his head.

311
00:20:59,920 --> 00:21:00,440
Show us some mercy.

312
00:21:03,420 --> 00:21:04,440
Drink some more of his wine.

313
00:21:04,680 --> 00:21:05,300
I'll take care of this.

314
00:21:06,480 --> 00:21:08,120
It's my father's hunting dog.

315
00:21:09,480 --> 00:21:11,700
He's only living to see Odysseus return.

316
00:21:12,080 --> 00:21:12,340
It's fine.

317
00:21:12,780 --> 00:21:16,060
I'll put him on the dung heap out front so
he can greet his master a little sooner.

318
00:21:26,500 --> 00:21:28,100
That dog stays out there!

319
00:21:30,060 --> 00:21:32,520
Next time think twice before we rough up
your life for an animal.

320
00:21:34,160 --> 00:21:34,600
I'll

321
00:21:48,450 --> 00:21:50,130
take them down to the ship, still no one.

322
00:21:50,570 --> 00:21:51,850
You're the only people you trust.

323
00:21:52,170 --> 00:21:53,630
You break your mother's heart.

324
00:21:53,630 --> 00:21:55,550
If I stay, they'll force me to fight.

325
00:21:56,070 --> 00:21:57,010
No one will back me.

326
00:21:57,430 --> 00:21:59,250
I have to find out what happened to my
father.

327
00:21:59,430 --> 00:22:02,770
Ithica will be behind me if they know
Odysseus is dead.

328
00:22:02,990 --> 00:22:04,330
The suitors will never allow that.

329
00:22:04,630 --> 00:22:05,710
You will never make it back.

330
00:22:05,870 --> 00:22:06,650
Tell them, T'or.

331
00:22:07,250 --> 00:22:09,030
Tell them to meet the ships just before
dawn.

332
00:22:09,610 --> 00:22:12,310
What do you want me to tell your mother?

333
00:22:13,350 --> 00:22:14,710
Hold out for as long as you can.

334
00:22:18,750 --> 00:22:19,410
Ship's ready.

335
00:22:19,950 --> 00:22:20,390
Tide's right.

336
00:22:20,390 --> 00:22:24,790
Have I ever told you that you have wise
eyes, Athena's eyes?

337
00:22:25,650 --> 00:22:26,270
Once or twice.

338
00:22:26,810 --> 00:22:28,490
You haven't even asked me where we're
going yet.

339
00:22:28,890 --> 00:22:29,610
Where are we going?

340
00:22:30,390 --> 00:22:31,230
We'll find my dad.

341
00:22:32,170 --> 00:22:32,730
He's alive.

342
00:22:32,890 --> 00:22:34,530
I can feel it in the wind and the waves.

343
00:22:34,670 --> 00:22:37,690
He's out there somewhere, wandering,
injured, lost.

344
00:22:37,790 --> 00:22:38,170
I don't know.

345
00:22:39,370 --> 00:22:40,150
But he's alive.

346
00:23:12,130 --> 00:23:12,870
More dreams?

347
00:23:14,310 --> 00:23:15,590
Men drowning.

348
00:23:16,790 --> 00:23:17,630
Inside.

349
00:23:20,370 --> 00:23:21,210
Some...

350
00:23:21,210 --> 00:23:22,010
dark space.

351
00:23:24,850 --> 00:23:26,390
Firelight on stone.

352
00:23:26,870 --> 00:23:27,310
I don't know.

353
00:23:29,990 --> 00:23:31,450
Don't you want to remember?

354
00:23:32,010 --> 00:23:34,310
Why can't God speak in ways we understand?

355
00:23:36,850 --> 00:23:38,730
Who doesn't understand, Bender?

356
00:23:39,990 --> 00:23:40,690
The fire.

357
00:23:40,690 --> 00:23:42,770
A child's smile.

358
00:23:43,170 --> 00:23:43,710
A good harvest.

359
00:23:44,710 --> 00:23:46,190
You hide yourselves from us.

360
00:23:47,970 --> 00:23:48,610
Look.

361
00:23:50,110 --> 00:23:51,190
We're smoothing that cloud.

362
00:23:52,410 --> 00:23:53,610
Massing those birds.

363
00:23:55,410 --> 00:23:56,410
We're smoothing the water.

364
00:23:58,570 --> 00:23:59,950
What were you talking to?

365
00:24:02,890 --> 00:24:04,530
Athena came to me again.

366
00:24:05,410 --> 00:24:06,550
She's telling me to remember.

367
00:24:08,570 --> 00:24:09,370
To leave.

368
00:24:09,990 --> 00:24:11,090
Why would you leave?

369
00:24:11,530 --> 00:24:12,910
We have everything we need.

370
00:24:13,930 --> 00:24:15,350
How long have I been here?

371
00:24:15,490 --> 00:24:15,870
Collapsed.

372
00:24:20,350 --> 00:24:22,170
I have a home somewhere.

373
00:24:25,150 --> 00:24:26,270
There's someone...

374
00:24:26,270 --> 00:24:26,550
out there.

375
00:24:27,530 --> 00:24:28,470
How do you know?

376
00:24:29,430 --> 00:24:30,190
I don't know.

377
00:24:32,910 --> 00:24:33,750
I feel.

378
00:24:50,070 --> 00:24:51,870
What we have here could last forever.

379
00:24:52,230 --> 00:24:53,390
Forever is for God.

380
00:24:53,390 --> 00:24:54,890
For your God.

381
00:24:57,490 --> 00:25:01,190
You want to remember, but what if
remembering destroys your happiness?

382
00:25:02,070 --> 00:25:02,970
Then it wasn't real.

383
00:25:11,260 --> 00:25:11,860
No.

384
00:25:12,580 --> 00:25:13,020
No more.

385
00:25:13,140 --> 00:25:13,640
But I love it.

386
00:25:14,400 --> 00:25:15,660
Stop eating the lotus.

387
00:25:16,600 --> 00:25:18,020
Tell me what you remember.

388
00:25:20,120 --> 00:25:21,020
After Troy.

389
00:25:23,120 --> 00:25:23,720
Troy.

390
00:25:30,140 --> 00:25:31,420
We won the war.

391
00:25:32,180 --> 00:25:33,100
And then what?

392
00:26:39,120 --> 00:26:41,080
Ten years on this fucking beach.

393
00:26:46,280 --> 00:26:47,260
Let's go home.

394
00:26:49,120 --> 00:26:50,480
Remember who we leave here.

395
00:26:55,290 --> 00:26:56,630
Do we have enough provisions?

396
00:26:57,690 --> 00:26:59,130
We've taken enough from Troy.

397
00:27:00,430 --> 00:27:01,910
Another stop or two won't kill us.

398
00:27:14,040 --> 00:27:15,080
It's still the oars.

399
00:27:25,430 --> 00:27:26,570
We'll raise the sail.

400
00:27:26,810 --> 00:27:27,930
Follow the southerly winds.

401
00:27:28,150 --> 00:27:28,450
Yes, sir.

402
00:27:30,350 --> 00:27:32,350
Agamemnon's rowing west on the known
provisions route.

403
00:27:32,610 --> 00:27:33,810
He's got more mouths to feed.

404
00:27:34,890 --> 00:27:36,130
We'll find places to limp.

405
00:27:36,670 --> 00:27:37,490
Are you sure it's a risk?

406
00:27:37,870 --> 00:27:39,410
That's all that Agamemnon long enough.

407
00:27:40,490 --> 00:27:45,270
With little luck and the right winds,
we'll see some of the world still be home

408
00:27:45,270 --> 00:27:46,130
before Agamemnon.

409
00:27:46,790 --> 00:27:47,470
Come on.

410
00:28:55,680 --> 00:28:57,660
Just keep the bear on the right.

411
00:29:07,840 --> 00:29:09,180
Oh, this is land!

412
00:29:13,130 --> 00:29:13,610
See?

413
00:29:14,850 --> 00:29:15,810
Lucky bastard.

414
00:29:16,690 --> 00:29:18,070
We're down to our last supplies.

415
00:29:18,710 --> 00:29:22,010
Assemble a landing party, food and water,
and up for three weeks.

416
00:29:22,650 --> 00:29:23,790
What if it won't give it?

417
00:29:25,690 --> 00:29:27,330
How badly do you want to get home?

418
00:29:28,390 --> 00:29:29,950
Get your weapons ready!

419
00:29:30,650 --> 00:29:31,730
Agamemnon, move in!

420
00:29:32,150 --> 00:29:32,510
Borrowing.

421
00:29:33,630 --> 00:29:41,660
Ask him why they abandoned their village
instead of welcoming us.

422
00:29:49,780 --> 00:29:50,500
No.

423
00:29:51,120 --> 00:29:52,060
We're Greeks.

424
00:29:53,080 --> 00:29:53,840
From Ithaca.

425
00:29:54,500 --> 00:29:56,200
Maybe you heard our victory at Troy.

426
00:29:57,300 --> 00:29:59,180
We're headed west on the southerly winds.

427
00:29:59,180 --> 00:30:02,300
If you get caught, you won't be able to
escape.

428
00:30:03,880 --> 00:30:06,160
You won't like what you'll find there.

429
00:30:06,460 --> 00:30:09,520
He says if we go that way, we won't like
the things we'll find.

430
00:30:11,220 --> 00:30:17,420
After years of war, no one could stand
between my men and home.

431
00:30:18,940 --> 00:30:19,680
Not even me.

432
00:30:21,480 --> 00:30:22,560
Then something happened.

433
00:30:39,110 --> 00:30:40,570
Chasing the escaping sun.

434
00:30:41,310 --> 00:30:42,010
Where?

435
00:30:43,990 --> 00:30:44,770
Where?

436
00:30:45,430 --> 00:30:46,790
There you go.

437
00:30:49,710 --> 00:30:51,070
Lucky bastard.

438
00:31:04,960 --> 00:31:07,440
Sheep coming flocks.

439
00:31:08,700 --> 00:31:10,180
Let's follow.

440
00:32:30,130 --> 00:32:31,990
Someone lives here.

441
00:32:33,690 --> 00:32:34,550
Yeah, they make cheese.

442
00:32:53,460 --> 00:32:54,280
Pretty good.

443
00:32:57,860 --> 00:32:59,000
There's a lot of it.

444
00:32:59,320 --> 00:32:59,920
We should leave.

445
00:33:00,860 --> 00:33:02,260
We brought nothing to trade with.

446
00:33:03,320 --> 00:33:04,000
Zeus' law.

447
00:33:04,500 --> 00:33:07,880
I don't know if whoever lives here has
heard of Zeus' law or Zeus, for that

448
00:33:07,880 --> 00:33:08,160
matter.

449
00:33:08,400 --> 00:33:09,800
Everyone's heard of Zeus' law.

450
00:33:10,520 --> 00:33:11,600
Get the fire going.

451
00:33:11,980 --> 00:33:13,140
We'll welcome the owner back.

452
00:34:32,620 --> 00:34:33,700
Get away from the fire.

453
00:34:34,760 --> 00:34:35,240
No!

454
00:34:35,500 --> 00:34:35,780
No!

455
00:36:12,770 --> 00:36:14,390
I think it's asleep.

456
00:36:59,770 --> 00:37:00,370
Wait!

457
00:37:04,120 --> 00:37:05,320
The rock.

458
00:37:13,080 --> 00:37:14,140
We can't move the rock.

459
00:37:14,500 --> 00:37:15,100
We'll be trapped.

460
00:37:23,900 --> 00:37:26,120
Spread the word in the morning.

461
00:37:26,600 --> 00:37:29,500
When it lets out its flock, we'll rush the
entrance.

462
00:37:30,280 --> 00:37:30,860
Pressed up.

463
00:38:17,450 --> 00:38:20,230
Can we jam something in the cave mouth?

464
00:38:20,770 --> 00:38:21,430
Leave a gap.

465
00:38:22,610 --> 00:38:23,090
Maybe.

466
00:38:24,310 --> 00:38:25,190
But what?

467
00:38:26,010 --> 00:38:26,670
There's law.

468
00:38:30,830 --> 00:38:31,570
It'll notice.

469
00:38:33,630 --> 00:38:34,910
But grab the thin end.

470
00:38:35,430 --> 00:38:36,110
Put it in the fire.

471
00:38:37,150 --> 00:38:38,530
How much rope do we have?

472
00:38:39,550 --> 00:38:40,350
How big is the flock?

473
00:38:40,650 --> 00:38:41,530
A couple dozen, maybe.

474
00:38:44,290 --> 00:38:45,370
Not too many of us.

475
00:38:46,270 --> 00:38:48,710
When it comes back, two of us have to get
out.

476
00:38:48,890 --> 00:38:49,950
Or at least get killed.

477
00:38:55,250 --> 00:38:58,490
Run low through the flock towards the
entrance and then split wide.

478
00:38:58,490 --> 00:39:00,450
So we can only grab one of them.

479
00:39:00,690 --> 00:39:00,810
Right.

480
00:39:01,350 --> 00:39:02,590
Four of us should make the run.

481
00:39:03,310 --> 00:39:03,730
Two and two.

482
00:39:06,570 --> 00:39:08,450
We're warming up the two before he sleeps.

483
00:41:12,820 --> 00:41:14,120
What about his body?

484
00:41:15,260 --> 00:41:15,880
What about it?

485
00:41:16,160 --> 00:41:17,260
We just leave him here to run.

486
00:41:18,720 --> 00:41:19,940
That's food for that thing?

487
00:41:21,940 --> 00:41:24,260
We honor our dead Odysseus.

488
00:41:24,260 --> 00:41:26,300
He died helping us escape.

489
00:41:26,540 --> 00:41:27,500
We'll honor him by escaping.

490
00:41:41,280 --> 00:41:42,980
He can talk.

491
00:41:44,480 --> 00:41:45,840
Why didn't he talk before?

492
00:41:47,740 --> 00:41:48,880
Do you talk to Hans?

493
00:41:50,480 --> 00:41:52,120
Should we reason with it?

494
00:41:54,720 --> 00:41:56,120
I think we're past that.

495
00:43:07,620 --> 00:43:08,340
What are you doing?

496
00:43:09,860 --> 00:43:10,220
No!

497
00:43:11,240 --> 00:43:11,680
Why

498
00:44:51,440 --> 00:44:53,460
do you have to fire that arrow?

499
00:44:53,920 --> 00:44:55,760
You lost good soldiers to that beast.

500
00:44:56,260 --> 00:44:59,000
Yeah, and that beast was a son up beside
him.

501
00:44:59,500 --> 00:44:59,780
What?

502
00:45:00,420 --> 00:45:02,740
When we put its eye out, we heard it
crying his name.

503
00:45:03,300 --> 00:45:04,300
That thing was a monster.

504
00:45:04,860 --> 00:45:05,820
I heard it, too.

505
00:45:06,940 --> 00:45:08,340
So now the sea is against us?

506
00:45:08,340 --> 00:45:09,600
And the wind.

507
00:45:10,860 --> 00:45:13,240
Are we supposed to get home with Poseidon
against us?

508
00:45:13,480 --> 00:45:15,460
We can't live by omens and sacrifices.

509
00:45:17,900 --> 00:45:21,780
Do you know how many times I've heard you
weeping, praying to Athena since we left

510
00:45:21,780 --> 00:45:22,060
Troy?

511
00:45:22,700 --> 00:45:23,800
Couldn't you have shown some mercy?

512
00:45:25,700 --> 00:45:26,400
Take this.

513
00:45:28,840 --> 00:45:29,540
Wear it.

514
00:45:30,260 --> 00:45:32,060
To remember those we couldn't bury.

515
00:45:33,740 --> 00:45:35,820
The gods help those who help themselves.

516
00:45:40,000 --> 00:45:44,940
From that moment, every rough sea or
headwind told the crew we were cursed.

517
00:45:47,580 --> 00:45:48,740
But where is my crew?

518
00:45:51,020 --> 00:45:52,560
When will you let me see my men?

519
00:45:53,800 --> 00:45:55,960
If I could just talk to them, they'd help
me remember.

520
00:45:56,560 --> 00:45:58,860
Did I have a wife, children, maybe a son?

521
00:45:59,600 --> 00:46:00,600
How long have I been here?

522
00:46:02,440 --> 00:46:03,320
A long time.

523
00:46:04,520 --> 00:46:09,060
If I had a son, how old would he be now?

524
00:46:11,320 --> 00:46:13,000
I don't know how to speak to a king.

525
00:46:13,580 --> 00:46:14,080
You'll be fine.

526
00:46:15,000 --> 00:46:15,820
Keep your mouth shut.

527
00:46:16,940 --> 00:46:21,520
But if anyone asks, we're traitors from
Crete, then we'll figure out how to

528
00:46:21,520 --> 00:46:22,240
approach the king.

529
00:46:33,720 --> 00:46:34,320
Thank you.

530
00:46:35,120 --> 00:46:36,160
You seem to be having quite...

531
00:46:36,160 --> 00:46:36,460
This way.

532
00:46:42,220 --> 00:46:43,300
Where are you taking us?

533
00:46:43,700 --> 00:46:45,940
The king insists on meeting strangers
himself.

534
00:47:07,880 --> 00:47:08,340
You sit.

535
00:47:12,260 --> 00:47:12,960
Not you.

536
00:47:15,100 --> 00:47:15,580
You.

537
00:47:18,100 --> 00:47:19,560
Enjoy our hospitality.

538
00:47:20,240 --> 00:47:21,020
As soon as it demands.

539
00:47:22,020 --> 00:47:23,640
But perhaps you'll tell us who you are.

540
00:47:24,940 --> 00:47:26,600
You picked the right day to arrive.

541
00:47:27,220 --> 00:47:28,380
Feast at my daughter's wedding.

542
00:47:29,200 --> 00:47:31,300
Tomorrow she leaves to start a new life.

543
00:47:32,640 --> 00:47:33,940
You don't look like a beggar.

544
00:47:34,580 --> 00:47:35,840
Imagine you know who I am.

545
00:47:37,480 --> 00:47:44,880
And now I asked the son of Atreus,
brother of Vagamemnon, a husband of Helen.

546
00:47:46,020 --> 00:47:47,100
Perhaps you've heard of Helen.

547
00:47:49,880 --> 00:47:50,520
Feast.

548
00:47:51,360 --> 00:47:52,980
I launched a thousand ships.

549
00:47:55,080 --> 00:47:56,880
That may be now just 500.

550
00:48:02,090 --> 00:48:04,930
Somebody left on the battlefields of Troy
for that face.

551
00:48:05,290 --> 00:48:07,010
For your brother's ambition.

552
00:48:07,010 --> 00:48:09,790
This one looks familiar.

553
00:48:10,930 --> 00:48:12,150
Hey, Jacks.

554
00:48:13,490 --> 00:48:14,170
Trocolus.

555
00:48:14,790 --> 00:48:15,130
Achilles.

556
00:48:16,130 --> 00:48:19,190
I don't miss him as much as one particular
warrior.

557
00:48:20,490 --> 00:48:21,850
He's the smartest of us all.

558
00:48:24,270 --> 00:48:26,530
Ten years we've rotted on that beach.

559
00:48:41,500 --> 00:48:43,860
Fighting amongst ourselves until one day.

560
00:48:46,920 --> 00:48:48,040
He found a way.

561
00:48:50,420 --> 00:48:50,860
Odysseus.

562
00:48:52,100 --> 00:48:53,680
Did you hear the story of the horse?

563
00:48:57,340 --> 00:48:58,960
I did hear it from the inside.

564
00:49:03,020 --> 00:49:04,740
We were in there for days.

565
00:49:06,120 --> 00:49:08,580
We squeezed inside before the ship set
sail.

566
00:49:10,380 --> 00:49:14,020
Odysseus, he knew that no one could know
that we were inside, not even sinning the

567
00:49:14,020 --> 00:49:15,160
soldier we left behind.

568
00:49:20,580 --> 00:49:23,960
The first two tides are men drowned right
in front of us.

569
00:49:29,000 --> 00:49:30,400
They're silent.

570
00:49:31,600 --> 00:49:32,540
They're pissed at shit.

571
00:50:00,320 --> 00:50:01,380
A gift.

572
00:50:06,070 --> 00:50:06,950
Common gods.

573
00:50:08,150 --> 00:50:09,190
Respect their offering.

574
00:54:48,980 --> 00:54:52,580
He knew the wait for a moonless night
around the plains of Troy.

575
00:54:53,680 --> 00:54:54,180
Filling

576
00:57:22,720 --> 00:57:22,980
it.

577
00:57:23,500 --> 00:57:24,540
You sure you're the bard singing?

578
00:57:25,260 --> 00:57:25,900
I have.

579
00:57:29,480 --> 00:57:31,280
But they weren't there, it was all of you.

580
00:57:31,700 --> 00:57:33,900
It was your father, Odysseus.

581
00:57:37,100 --> 00:57:38,220
You're so like him.

582
00:57:39,300 --> 00:57:40,740
When did you last see him?

583
00:57:41,420 --> 00:57:41,860
Troy.

584
00:57:43,180 --> 00:57:43,680
God's will.

585
00:57:43,680 --> 00:57:45,100
God's granted us a righteous victory.

586
00:57:45,360 --> 00:57:45,880
Righteous.

587
00:57:47,020 --> 00:57:50,960
The gods granted our righteous victory,
but did not bless our voyages.

588
00:57:52,100 --> 00:57:53,720
Took me years to get home.

589
00:57:54,800 --> 00:57:55,300
And he didn't.

590
00:57:55,720 --> 00:57:57,320
Some did, in which they hadn't.

591
00:57:58,180 --> 00:57:58,920
Tell him.

592
00:57:59,900 --> 00:58:01,160
Tell him about your brother.

593
00:58:01,740 --> 00:58:05,040
About the fate that the gods bestowed on
him after your righteous struggle.

594
00:58:05,780 --> 00:58:06,860
About his heroes welcome.

595
00:58:07,220 --> 00:58:10,400
About how Agamemnon made it home years
before us.

596
00:58:10,400 --> 00:58:14,120
Tell him how he was welcomed by his queen,
my twin sister, Clytemnestra.

597
00:58:14,520 --> 00:58:15,220
No, no.

598
00:58:15,480 --> 00:58:16,200
Tell him how she...

599
00:58:16,200 --> 00:58:16,440
No!

600
00:58:17,040 --> 00:58:18,680
He does not want to hear about my brother!

601
00:58:21,840 --> 00:58:23,020
What's the news of his father?

602
00:58:24,140 --> 00:58:26,660
I know nothing of Odysseus, since Troy.

603
00:58:26,760 --> 00:58:27,080
Nothing?

604
00:58:28,780 --> 00:58:30,340
Not from any of your men.

605
00:58:31,540 --> 00:58:33,680
Our Agamemmons were traitors and
travelers.

606
00:58:33,980 --> 00:58:35,020
Did you come here for summers?

607
00:58:35,020 --> 00:58:36,720
Is that what you're interested in,
gossip?

608
00:58:37,820 --> 00:58:38,840
You're in the right place.

609
00:58:38,840 --> 00:58:41,860
Anyone want to spin a story about
Odysseus?

610
00:58:42,720 --> 00:58:45,000
Some say he's rich or some say poor.

611
00:58:46,300 --> 00:58:48,980
Some say he perished, some say he's
imprisoned.

612
00:58:49,260 --> 00:58:49,920
What say you?

613
00:58:50,140 --> 00:58:50,740
Imprisoned.

614
00:58:53,580 --> 00:58:54,900
What kind of prison?

615
00:58:56,000 --> 00:58:57,300
An old man like that.

616
00:58:58,760 --> 00:59:00,360
Tell me what you do remember.

617
00:59:01,300 --> 00:59:02,120
The rest will come.

618
00:59:03,560 --> 00:59:07,000
And the man became more and more convinced
that I defended Poseidon.

619
00:59:52,760 --> 00:59:57,940
We were hit by storm after storm,
all pushing us off course, until we were

620
00:59:57,940 --> 00:59:58,860
truly lost.

621
01:00:51,380 --> 01:00:52,020
Hello.

622
01:00:54,360 --> 01:00:55,600
We're here in peace.

623
01:00:58,410 --> 01:00:59,950
We just need some food.

624
01:01:05,790 --> 01:01:07,170
We need food!

625
01:01:08,730 --> 01:01:10,230
Can you understand that?

626
01:01:10,330 --> 01:01:10,930
It's just a child.

627
01:01:13,970 --> 01:01:14,950
Is that a child?

628
01:01:15,130 --> 01:01:15,870
What about its parents?

629
01:01:18,730 --> 01:01:20,250
Hey, shh, it's okay.

630
01:01:21,390 --> 01:01:21,990
No, no, no, no.

631
01:01:22,290 --> 01:01:22,550
Shhh!

632
01:01:44,600 --> 01:01:45,660
Back to the ship!

633
01:05:27,300 --> 01:05:30,020
Turn to port, follow the coast.

634
01:05:31,020 --> 01:05:32,060
What if they have ships?

635
01:05:32,420 --> 01:05:34,380
We need to get as far away as possible.

636
01:05:38,800 --> 01:05:40,480
We don't have supplies for a block.

637
01:05:40,480 --> 01:05:41,040
We need to find crossing.

638
01:05:41,720 --> 01:05:43,020
We've lost our way.

639
01:05:43,740 --> 01:05:46,560
We need to find food and get our bearings.

640
01:05:49,460 --> 01:05:50,500
Turn to port.

641
01:06:17,020 --> 01:06:17,700
There!

642
01:06:18,560 --> 01:06:19,540
Come on, there!

643
01:06:20,400 --> 01:06:22,920
Go past, we'll find anchorage at the
beach.

644
01:06:30,570 --> 01:06:31,370
We're done!

645
01:06:35,850 --> 01:06:37,050
Form a landing party.

646
01:06:40,090 --> 01:06:40,310
Tomorrow.

647
01:06:43,110 --> 01:06:43,630
Tomorrow.

648
01:06:54,650 --> 01:06:56,170
Leave someone with the tenders.

649
01:06:57,370 --> 01:06:58,850
We think you should stay.

650
01:07:01,310 --> 01:07:01,830
We?

651
01:07:02,190 --> 01:07:02,930
All of us.

652
01:07:03,870 --> 01:07:04,610
Still alive.

653
01:07:15,690 --> 01:07:16,810
Is this a mutiny?

654
01:07:17,510 --> 01:07:18,270
That's up to you.

655
01:07:23,040 --> 01:07:23,920
Give me your boat.

656
01:07:24,400 --> 01:07:25,460
I'll make sure we eat tonight.

657
01:07:26,400 --> 01:07:27,420
No matter what you find.

658
01:09:14,520 --> 01:09:15,720
No, no, no, it's okay!

659
01:09:16,000 --> 01:09:16,420
It's okay!

660
01:09:21,620 --> 01:09:22,380
You're okay!

661
01:09:22,720 --> 01:09:23,540
It's all right!

662
01:09:23,640 --> 01:09:24,400
No one's gonna hurt you!

663
01:09:24,720 --> 01:09:25,980
If I do what you want.

664
01:09:26,200 --> 01:09:28,180
No one is going to hurt you.

665
01:09:32,000 --> 01:09:33,280
My name's your Ilocus.

666
01:09:33,360 --> 01:09:33,820
What's your name?

667
01:09:34,380 --> 01:09:34,880
Cersei.

668
01:09:35,420 --> 01:09:35,920
Cersei?

669
01:09:36,500 --> 01:09:37,340
We need food.

670
01:09:37,560 --> 01:09:38,060
Badly.

671
01:09:39,120 --> 01:09:40,100
We have food.

672
01:09:42,180 --> 01:09:42,680
Lucky.

673
01:09:42,680 --> 01:09:43,360
It's food.

674
01:10:04,820 --> 01:10:05,780
You let me.

675
01:10:08,680 --> 01:10:10,000
Your weapons.

676
01:10:10,460 --> 01:10:10,740
We're armor.

677
01:10:12,500 --> 01:10:13,560
Put it out the back.

678
01:10:57,030 --> 01:10:58,030
Do you like my meat?

679
01:10:59,010 --> 01:10:59,470
Why?

680
01:10:59,950 --> 01:11:01,310
Do you have so much?

681
01:11:02,230 --> 01:11:04,650
You never know when strangers will come.

682
01:11:18,640 --> 01:11:19,800
What's their appetite?

683
01:11:22,140 --> 01:11:23,580
Too much is never enough.

684
01:12:40,650 --> 01:12:41,090
Ilocus!

685
01:12:44,490 --> 01:12:44,930
Don't

686
01:13:08,760 --> 01:13:08,860
move!

687
01:13:09,160 --> 01:13:09,380
No!

688
01:13:09,840 --> 01:13:10,380
Give me your dagger.

689
01:13:14,340 --> 01:13:15,200
Stay with the boat!

690
01:13:56,570 --> 01:13:58,010
Why don't you just come here?

691
01:14:06,460 --> 01:14:08,140
Did you come from the sea?

692
01:14:10,040 --> 01:14:10,960
Are you alone?

693
01:14:16,520 --> 01:14:18,880
My pigs have taken a liking to you.

694
01:14:20,060 --> 01:14:20,860
Are you hungry?

695
01:14:23,440 --> 01:14:25,100
Leave your weapons at the door.

696
01:14:25,520 --> 01:14:27,120
Enjoy the hospitality of Zeus.

697
01:14:29,080 --> 01:14:30,020
Grab a hog.

698
01:14:30,240 --> 01:14:31,480
You can slaughter it for your dinner.

699
01:14:56,860 --> 01:14:59,320
Whoever you are, you're clearly not a
farmer.

700
01:15:00,160 --> 01:15:00,360
Here.

701
01:15:04,260 --> 01:15:05,600
That stew smells good.

702
01:15:09,440 --> 01:15:10,780
That's enough for just us.

703
01:15:12,300 --> 01:15:13,780
Who says it's just us?

704
01:15:15,720 --> 01:15:16,820
Who else is here?

705
01:15:17,560 --> 01:15:18,140
Who indeed.

706
01:15:31,720 --> 01:15:33,300
You say you're all alone.

707
01:15:34,040 --> 01:15:35,460
But no one comes here alone.

708
01:15:36,840 --> 01:15:37,160
No?

709
01:15:38,040 --> 01:15:40,340
You can only reach this spot by sea.

710
01:15:41,260 --> 01:15:41,500
So how do you get there?

711
01:15:41,500 --> 01:15:43,100
So where are your shipmates?

712
01:15:44,340 --> 01:15:45,300
I'm looking for them.

713
01:15:46,560 --> 01:15:48,180
Anyone happy to come this way?

714
01:15:51,280 --> 01:15:52,880
But you've prepared all this food.

715
01:15:53,220 --> 01:15:54,180
You're questioning me?

716
01:15:54,840 --> 01:15:55,100
You?

717
01:15:57,360 --> 01:15:59,440
A traveler who's clearly starving.

718
01:15:59,960 --> 01:16:01,860
It hasn't touched the food in front of
him.

719
01:16:02,280 --> 01:16:03,600
Claims to be a sailor.

720
01:16:03,880 --> 01:16:05,380
Yet we're as a great warrior's armor.

721
01:16:06,520 --> 01:16:08,200
And who breaks Zeus' law.

722
01:16:08,920 --> 01:16:11,580
By bringing a dagger into his hostess's
house.

723
01:16:12,120 --> 01:16:13,200
You have sharp eyes.

724
01:16:14,040 --> 01:16:15,140
Sharpened by the gods.

725
01:16:16,040 --> 01:16:17,300
Do they grant you insights?

726
01:16:18,920 --> 01:16:19,460
Truths.

727
01:16:20,680 --> 01:16:21,220
Sometimes.

728
01:16:22,340 --> 01:16:24,220
Would they tell you where I could find my
men?

729
01:16:25,260 --> 01:16:25,840
Your men.

730
01:16:26,420 --> 01:16:26,920
That's right.

731
01:16:27,920 --> 01:16:28,700
They would.

732
01:16:29,340 --> 01:16:30,280
Then would you tell me?

733
01:16:31,380 --> 01:16:31,920
Perhaps.

734
01:16:34,060 --> 01:16:35,460
If you give me your dagger.

735
01:16:45,660 --> 01:16:47,620
Fire refuses my hospitality.

736
01:17:13,490 --> 01:17:14,650
Leaving their rings.

737
01:17:25,970 --> 01:17:26,490
Don't!

738
01:17:27,050 --> 01:17:28,490
I don't know who this crow is to you.

739
01:17:28,970 --> 01:17:29,990
You don't put that knife down.

740
01:17:30,350 --> 01:17:31,730
I will throttle it to find out.

741
01:17:32,270 --> 01:17:32,970
Don't hurt him.

742
01:17:33,270 --> 01:17:33,650
Family.

743
01:17:36,190 --> 01:17:36,710
Mother.

744
01:17:39,610 --> 01:17:40,490
Your sister.

745
01:17:44,330 --> 01:17:47,330
You did this to her like you turned my men
into piths.

746
01:17:47,330 --> 01:17:48,990
I didn't turn your men into anything.

747
01:17:49,690 --> 01:17:50,330
Look at them.

748
01:17:50,670 --> 01:17:51,570
This is who they are.

749
01:17:51,710 --> 01:17:52,230
Look at them.

750
01:17:52,910 --> 01:17:53,390
Base.

751
01:17:54,330 --> 01:17:54,810
Disgusting.

752
01:17:55,390 --> 01:17:57,530
Primal urges that you value in battle.

753
01:17:58,010 --> 01:18:00,650
They've raped and pillaged across the
world for you.

754
01:18:01,130 --> 01:18:02,430
And you think that's against their nature?

755
01:18:02,710 --> 01:18:04,730
You think they only did that because you
ordered them to?

756
01:18:05,330 --> 01:18:08,530
A true commander thinks his men's savagery
is actually discipline.

757
01:18:09,510 --> 01:18:11,230
If you don't seem that deluded.

758
01:18:11,230 --> 01:18:13,550
But you sent them to me here.

759
01:18:13,910 --> 01:18:14,770
On my own.

760
01:18:15,450 --> 01:18:18,830
A gang of filthy soldiers with empty
bellies and hot blood.

761
01:18:20,850 --> 01:18:23,650
You thought they'd respect me and my home?

762
01:18:24,710 --> 01:18:25,710
Look at them.

763
01:18:26,290 --> 01:18:27,430
Know your own men.

764
01:18:28,650 --> 01:18:29,810
Did they hurt you?

765
01:18:30,470 --> 01:18:31,430
They would have.

766
01:18:32,170 --> 01:18:33,750
They just want to go home.

767
01:18:37,370 --> 01:18:38,210
Let them.

768
01:19:26,230 --> 01:19:27,290
Come back.

769
01:19:27,950 --> 01:19:31,810
Back into your disguise.

770
01:19:38,390 --> 01:19:38,810
Odysseus.

771
01:19:39,770 --> 01:19:40,690
Back to the ship.

772
01:19:41,170 --> 01:19:42,650
Take all the food you can carry.

773
01:19:43,550 --> 01:19:44,230
Leave the meat.

774
01:19:45,050 --> 01:19:45,950
You're not going to let her live.

775
01:19:46,130 --> 01:19:46,650
She's a witch.

776
01:19:47,490 --> 01:19:48,530
Who gave her powers?

777
01:19:49,390 --> 01:19:49,890
Your gods.

778
01:19:50,730 --> 01:19:52,130
Fill the jugs and skins of the stream.

779
01:19:52,550 --> 01:19:53,550
Be ready to sail at dawn.

780
01:19:59,810 --> 01:20:00,930
Well, I leave her like this.

781
01:20:01,710 --> 01:20:03,130
We get on better this way.

782
01:20:04,790 --> 01:20:06,470
I'm sorry if we offended you.

783
01:20:07,930 --> 01:20:09,690
We can't get back without your help.

784
01:20:10,550 --> 01:20:12,030
We need to know the way to the trade
routes.

785
01:20:12,410 --> 01:20:13,350
You're too lost.

786
01:20:14,090 --> 01:20:14,650
What do you mean?

787
01:20:16,570 --> 01:20:19,430
Only blind Thereseus can tell you how to
go home.

788
01:20:19,930 --> 01:20:20,450
Thereseus?

789
01:20:20,770 --> 01:20:21,330
In Hades.

790
01:20:22,250 --> 01:20:23,750
Nobody sails into hell.

791
01:20:24,430 --> 01:20:25,310
Then comes back.

792
01:20:25,950 --> 01:20:26,470
You will.

793
01:20:27,730 --> 01:20:30,030
Head north into the twilight.

794
01:20:35,620 --> 01:20:40,380
And head inland until the river of fire
meets the river of ice.

795
01:21:30,860 --> 01:21:32,660
There you must dig a trench.

796
01:21:35,340 --> 01:21:37,680
And fill it with the blood of sacrifice.

797
01:21:57,990 --> 01:21:59,150
Gather the dead.

798
01:22:00,470 --> 01:22:02,370
And learn how you go home.

799
01:22:04,450 --> 01:22:05,130
You.

800
01:22:06,590 --> 01:22:07,790
Not your men.

801
01:22:08,910 --> 01:22:09,810
You curse them?

802
01:22:10,570 --> 01:22:11,510
Not a curse.

803
01:22:12,730 --> 01:22:13,590
A truth.

804
01:22:15,190 --> 01:22:17,330
Your men don't want you to defy the gods.

805
01:22:19,390 --> 01:22:21,610
The gods say only you make it home.

806
01:22:22,810 --> 01:22:24,190
Then I defy the gods.

807
01:23:05,350 --> 01:23:06,030
Sinai.

808
01:23:07,150 --> 01:23:07,990
It's Odysseus.

809
01:23:12,990 --> 01:23:13,670
Odysseus.

810
01:23:13,670 --> 01:23:13,830
Sinai.

811
01:23:15,370 --> 01:23:16,270
It's Odysseus.

812
01:23:18,790 --> 01:23:20,530
Let him drink Odysseus.

813
01:23:21,350 --> 01:23:22,930
Then you will hear his testimony.

814
01:23:38,130 --> 01:23:38,810
Odysseus.

815
01:23:39,310 --> 01:23:39,610
Yes.

816
01:23:40,590 --> 01:23:42,170
He lied to everyone.

817
01:23:45,850 --> 01:23:47,490
Did you have to lie to me?

818
01:23:49,270 --> 01:23:50,470
I needed you to believe.

819
01:23:52,410 --> 01:23:53,990
Why did you have to volunteer?

820
01:23:55,230 --> 01:23:56,690
Don't you remember how I came here?

821
01:23:57,030 --> 01:23:59,550
I begged to take Intinuous's place.

822
01:24:00,230 --> 01:24:00,870
He'd been called.

823
01:24:01,930 --> 01:24:04,610
He offered money for your family if you
take his place.

824
01:24:05,810 --> 01:24:07,850
If the Trojans don't let me live.

825
01:24:09,790 --> 01:24:11,210
If I never make it home.

826
01:24:12,030 --> 01:24:13,570
Take this back to my father.

827
01:24:15,270 --> 01:24:16,490
Tell him everything I did.

828
01:24:20,210 --> 01:24:20,770
Odysseus.

829
01:24:22,750 --> 01:24:24,890
Feel free to add some stuff.

830
01:24:28,270 --> 01:24:30,390
You didn't trust me with the truth?

831
01:24:32,350 --> 01:24:34,010
You knew I'd die for you.

832
01:24:34,470 --> 01:24:36,990
Dying meant the truth I needed you to
believe.

833
01:24:37,690 --> 01:24:39,970
I died for your lie on that beach.

834
01:24:40,950 --> 01:24:41,710
I know.

835
01:24:42,290 --> 01:24:43,390
I was there with you.

836
01:24:43,990 --> 01:24:46,510
I heard you repeat the lie with your dying
breath.

837
01:24:46,930 --> 01:24:48,030
Winning us the war.

838
01:24:48,030 --> 01:24:51,270
I was right there, inches from you,
smelling your blood.

839
01:24:51,470 --> 01:24:53,070
What comfort could that give me?

840
01:24:54,690 --> 01:24:58,490
Not knowing you were inside that horse
until the dead told me.

841
01:24:59,710 --> 01:25:00,270
Which dead?

842
01:25:01,190 --> 01:25:04,110
A lot of veterans of your campaigns walk
these shores.

843
01:25:06,530 --> 01:25:11,450
The dead meet to lament the living and
greet the newly dead.

844
01:25:12,130 --> 01:25:14,770
My father was the first shade to find me.

845
01:25:15,370 --> 01:25:16,070
To comfort you?

846
01:25:16,070 --> 01:25:17,950
Shades don't comfort.

847
01:25:18,950 --> 01:25:22,230
They want word of the living who wronged
them.

848
01:25:23,130 --> 01:25:25,250
My father had died in poverty.

849
01:25:25,730 --> 01:25:30,110
Alone, begging for scraps from people like
Antinous.

850
01:25:30,790 --> 01:25:32,090
Antinous cheated you?

851
01:25:33,050 --> 01:25:34,870
As he's no doubt cheating you.

852
01:25:35,550 --> 01:25:35,830
How?

853
01:25:36,230 --> 01:25:38,750
Who's inethica looking after your wife and
son?

854
01:25:39,230 --> 01:25:40,530
My wife is of no concern.

855
01:25:41,990 --> 01:25:44,610
Talk to Agamonon about homecomings.

856
01:25:46,170 --> 01:25:46,890
Agamonon's here?

857
01:25:50,070 --> 01:25:52,330
You buried me at Troy with respect.

858
01:25:52,830 --> 01:25:53,450
Thank you.

859
01:25:54,370 --> 01:25:56,270
But spare a thought for those you didn't.

860
01:25:56,910 --> 01:25:58,350
It's not always possible.

861
01:25:59,290 --> 01:26:05,050
When you get home, take a ship,
head into the unknown west till you find

862
01:26:05,050 --> 01:26:05,550
land.

863
01:26:06,070 --> 01:26:09,450
And there, give sacrifice and honor your
men.

864
01:26:11,750 --> 01:26:13,210
Do you still have the lot?

865
01:26:16,900 --> 01:26:17,640
Of course.

866
01:26:18,660 --> 01:26:21,800
If you make it home, give Antinous back
his shame.

867
01:26:22,460 --> 01:26:22,840
I will.

868
01:26:34,400 --> 01:26:35,040
My lord.

869
01:26:47,390 --> 01:26:47,870
Odysseus.

870
01:26:49,050 --> 01:26:49,970
How are you here?

871
01:26:50,910 --> 01:26:52,670
We won the war, you headed for home.

872
01:26:53,850 --> 01:26:54,330
Odysseus.

873
01:26:55,010 --> 01:26:56,730
The most brilliant of all my generals.

874
01:26:58,190 --> 01:27:00,090
He would never be snared as I was.

875
01:27:00,270 --> 01:27:01,810
Will you cast a drift, like us?

876
01:27:02,570 --> 01:27:03,150
No.

877
01:27:03,730 --> 01:27:05,370
My misfortune was to make it home.

878
01:27:07,030 --> 01:27:08,730
You'll be welcomed by my beautiful wife.

879
01:27:15,460 --> 01:27:16,040
Bathed.

880
01:27:18,740 --> 01:27:19,320
Anointed.

881
01:27:21,980 --> 01:27:23,080
Made soft.

882
01:27:24,420 --> 01:27:26,000
To learn the hardest lesson.

883
01:27:27,100 --> 01:27:29,260
I'd left a poison in her heart years
before.

884
01:27:31,880 --> 01:27:33,520
A good part of her heart had gone.

885
01:27:40,040 --> 01:27:42,720
She and her lover had plotted my murder
for years.

886
01:27:44,680 --> 01:27:45,240
Odysseus.

887
01:27:46,420 --> 01:27:46,980
Yes.

888
01:27:47,980 --> 01:27:49,100
My son Arrestes.

889
01:27:49,780 --> 01:27:50,900
Did he avenge me?

890
01:27:51,580 --> 01:27:52,640
My lord, how could I know?

891
01:27:53,940 --> 01:27:55,200
I'm still trying to get home.

892
01:27:56,500 --> 01:27:57,720
Don't make my mistake.

893
01:27:59,040 --> 01:28:02,020
Don't walk on the front door expecting
garlands and praise.

894
01:28:03,020 --> 01:28:03,860
Come in disguise.

895
01:28:05,100 --> 01:28:05,980
Take your time.

896
01:28:07,220 --> 01:28:07,780
It's us.

897
01:28:15,290 --> 01:28:16,090
Do you see?

898
01:28:18,150 --> 01:28:19,470
I know what I have to do.

899
01:28:20,070 --> 01:28:21,550
Some of what you have to do.

900
01:28:21,970 --> 01:28:23,710
My prophecy is not for the Ariers.

901
01:28:25,390 --> 01:28:26,290
Back to the ship.

902
01:28:28,910 --> 01:28:31,130
Sail southwest past the sirens.

903
01:28:32,610 --> 01:28:34,930
Their song glures your men onto the rocks.

904
01:28:35,390 --> 01:28:35,950
They all die.

905
01:28:36,410 --> 01:28:37,350
I won't let that happen.

906
01:28:37,990 --> 01:28:40,010
I'll blot their ears with wax.

907
01:28:40,790 --> 01:28:45,730
Past the sirens, you choose between the
Whirlpool Caribdis and the monster Scylla.

908
01:28:46,330 --> 01:28:48,570
The Whirlpool in the sea takes all of your
lives.

909
01:28:48,930 --> 01:28:50,750
The monster in the cliffs, only six.

910
01:28:51,390 --> 01:28:55,510
But your determination to not sacrifice
any more lives kills all of them.

911
01:28:56,510 --> 01:28:57,590
I'll do what I have to.

912
01:28:57,910 --> 01:29:02,450
At the end, when starvation and mutiny
threaten, they demand to land where the

913
01:29:02,450 --> 01:29:03,950
sun god grazes his cattle.

914
01:29:03,950 --> 01:29:06,150
They slaughter Apollo's herd.

915
01:29:07,490 --> 01:29:09,210
Zeus kills them for it.

916
01:29:09,750 --> 01:29:10,450
Do you see?

917
01:29:11,550 --> 01:29:12,710
I'll stop them.

918
01:29:13,290 --> 01:29:16,590
Why do you value these men's lives more
than those you've already lost?

919
01:29:16,890 --> 01:29:18,490
I can still save them from the gods.

920
01:29:19,610 --> 01:29:21,090
But not from themselves.

921
01:29:23,990 --> 01:29:24,950
What is that?

922
01:29:25,430 --> 01:29:26,530
You are a buried crew.

923
01:29:30,390 --> 01:29:32,390
Demanding you share their dishonor.

924
01:29:34,510 --> 01:29:35,530
Let's go!

925
01:30:50,060 --> 01:30:52,280
Leaving so many fallen comrades.

926
01:30:53,160 --> 01:30:54,920
I have to save the rest.

927
01:30:55,820 --> 01:30:57,120
What's happened to them?

928
01:30:57,960 --> 01:30:59,260
Did they leave me here?

929
01:30:59,740 --> 01:31:00,720
Are they coming back?

930
01:31:02,920 --> 01:31:04,700
Did you do something to them?

931
01:31:06,700 --> 01:31:07,860
Did you do something to them?

932
01:31:08,280 --> 01:31:08,700
I'm sorry.

933
01:31:09,440 --> 01:31:11,640
You told me not to eat the lotus flower.

934
01:31:12,040 --> 01:31:12,740
Just tonight.

935
01:31:13,580 --> 01:31:15,080
So you can get some sleep.

936
01:31:18,470 --> 01:31:21,650
And tomorrow, when you're ready,
you'll remember.

937
01:31:24,970 --> 01:31:26,510
You'll remember your crew.

938
01:31:27,670 --> 01:31:28,450
And your family.

939
01:31:29,010 --> 01:31:29,350
Family.

940
01:31:31,510 --> 01:31:32,410
A wife.

941
01:31:34,470 --> 01:31:35,810
Do I have a wife?

942
01:31:41,570 --> 01:31:43,110
So I'm a lad, though.

943
01:31:43,990 --> 01:31:45,010
Strangers don't come in here.

944
01:31:45,570 --> 01:31:46,710
Screen or no screen.

945
01:31:46,710 --> 01:31:49,450
After three years of feasting together,
we're far from strangers.

946
01:31:49,690 --> 01:31:53,010
Are you trying to marry me or ruin my
reputation?

947
01:31:53,590 --> 01:31:54,670
Melanto can be discreet.

948
01:31:55,050 --> 01:31:58,370
I'm actually here out of concern for your
reputation.

949
01:31:58,970 --> 01:31:59,470
Oh?

950
01:31:59,930 --> 01:32:01,410
Everyone sees how hard you work.

951
01:32:01,830 --> 01:32:03,910
So steady progress.

952
01:32:04,590 --> 01:32:07,550
But every night, you've been unraveling
that work.

953
01:32:13,390 --> 01:32:14,190
Leave us.

954
01:32:16,190 --> 01:32:20,030
You can't expect her to be loyal to a
master she never even knew.

955
01:32:20,030 --> 01:32:21,990
I expect her to be loyal to me.

956
01:32:22,430 --> 01:32:23,070
Then she will be.

957
01:32:23,570 --> 01:32:24,370
Then you marry to me.

958
01:32:26,670 --> 01:32:29,090
Slaves have no choice but to be loyal to
the future.

959
01:32:29,710 --> 01:32:31,630
And this is a household waiting for a
master.

960
01:32:32,250 --> 01:32:34,110
Just as Ethica's a king and waiting for a
king.

961
01:32:37,030 --> 01:32:38,350
Ethica's king is coming back.

962
01:32:39,130 --> 01:32:39,750
No, he's not.

963
01:32:41,750 --> 01:32:44,550
Do you know that as a boy, I tried to go
with him to Troy?

964
01:32:45,910 --> 01:32:46,890
He never told me that.

965
01:32:48,450 --> 01:32:49,990
This is refused to let me.

966
01:32:50,310 --> 01:32:53,290
He knew that Ethica needed a future
without him.

967
01:32:59,940 --> 01:33:01,860
And he saw me as part of that future.

968
01:33:06,760 --> 01:33:10,120
I'll never tell other suitors about
your...

969
01:33:11,000 --> 01:33:11,400
perception.

970
01:33:14,440 --> 01:33:15,540
Finish your weaving.

971
01:33:17,700 --> 01:33:18,920
It's time to let him go.

972
01:33:28,380 --> 01:33:31,020
I wish you wouldn't marry someone who
loves someone else.

973
01:33:33,900 --> 01:33:38,700
Your loyalty to Odysseus is one of your
finest qualities.

974
01:33:41,520 --> 01:33:43,200
I love you.

975
01:33:44,180 --> 01:33:47,220
I want you to choose me and end all this.

976
01:33:48,660 --> 01:33:51,160
You can't tell me you're not tired.

977
01:33:52,240 --> 01:33:54,560
The waiting, the uncertainty.

978
01:33:57,140 --> 01:33:59,400
You've done your duty.

979
01:34:00,820 --> 01:34:04,120
Year after year after year.

980
01:34:07,160 --> 01:34:08,540
It's time to think of yourself.

981
01:34:10,180 --> 01:34:11,680
It's time to live again.

982
01:34:17,640 --> 01:34:18,900
That's what Odysseus would have wanted.

983
01:34:25,780 --> 01:34:27,620
I'll take care of all your needs.

984
01:34:36,370 --> 01:34:37,370
And my son?

985
01:34:39,070 --> 01:34:40,430
The throne as he is.

986
01:34:41,110 --> 01:34:41,930
He's the heir.

987
01:34:44,590 --> 01:34:46,270
Unless we have a child of our own.

988
01:34:56,660 --> 01:34:57,640
Where is Telemachus?

989
01:34:59,900 --> 01:35:02,560
He's gone inland to visit his grandfather.

990
01:35:28,380 --> 01:35:29,840
He's gone for years with his father.

991
01:35:31,000 --> 01:35:31,400
Where?

992
01:35:32,140 --> 01:35:33,200
He's gone to Sparta.

993
01:35:33,200 --> 01:35:34,420
To Minalaus.

994
01:35:35,720 --> 01:35:36,740
Well, should we go there?

995
01:35:38,380 --> 01:35:38,820
No.

996
01:35:40,660 --> 01:35:41,920
Too loud, Melantis.

997
01:35:42,160 --> 01:35:43,280
I told you you're a coward!

998
01:35:44,980 --> 01:35:45,860
He was listening!

999
01:35:45,960 --> 01:35:46,400
No, no!

1000
01:35:49,440 --> 01:35:51,160
I was coming for Argus.

1001
01:35:52,600 --> 01:35:54,120
I told you that dog stays there.

1002
01:35:54,420 --> 01:35:55,140
Go back to the farm.

1003
01:35:55,480 --> 01:35:56,860
When we need your pigs, we'll send
Melantis.

1004
01:35:58,340 --> 01:36:00,460
You're no longer welcome in this palace,
old man.

1005
01:36:00,460 --> 01:36:02,220
My answer to my mistress.

1006
01:36:04,840 --> 01:36:06,020
Does she even know your name?

1007
01:36:07,940 --> 01:36:08,260
Now go.

1008
01:36:09,860 --> 01:36:12,840
Telemachus will have to come back by the
temple at Pylos.

1009
01:36:13,500 --> 01:36:14,780
You take your man away from there.

1010
01:36:15,760 --> 01:36:17,440
Well, as you can stay and try and charm
the queen.

1011
01:36:17,920 --> 01:36:18,500
I don't think so.

1012
01:36:19,700 --> 01:36:20,960
He needs to die on that road.

1013
01:36:21,780 --> 01:36:22,600
And Iris has a plan.

1014
01:36:24,020 --> 01:36:24,420
Iris!

1015
01:36:27,020 --> 01:36:29,780
I need everyone and his party dead in a
ditch.

1016
01:36:30,360 --> 01:36:31,140
Throat's open.

1017
01:36:31,720 --> 01:36:35,100
Telemachus and his men will be ready for
the bandits who work the road between here

1018
01:36:35,100 --> 01:36:35,740
and Pylos.

1019
01:36:36,900 --> 01:36:40,420
But if some of them took the temple from
the priests before they arrived,

1020
01:36:41,820 --> 01:36:43,600
then bring me part of Telemachus' proof.

1021
01:36:45,660 --> 01:36:46,120
His nose.

1022
01:36:47,240 --> 01:36:48,600
Would you know him only by his nose?

1023
01:36:52,360 --> 01:36:53,280
His ears, too.

1024
01:36:58,610 --> 01:36:59,850
Who taught you?

1025
01:37:00,550 --> 01:37:01,610
My father's swine herd.

1026
01:37:02,330 --> 01:37:02,910
A swine herd?

1027
01:37:03,610 --> 01:37:04,710
Asma's loyal servant.

1028
01:37:05,790 --> 01:37:08,190
Odysseus left his most loyal servant
behind.

1029
01:37:08,910 --> 01:37:09,930
He always was the smartest.

1030
01:37:11,190 --> 01:37:13,150
Only one Agamemnon ever listened to.

1031
01:37:14,230 --> 01:37:15,430
Should have listened harder.

1032
01:37:16,130 --> 01:37:17,670
Can I ask what happened to your brother?

1033
01:37:18,190 --> 01:37:19,270
His wife betrayed him.

1034
01:37:19,730 --> 01:37:20,250
He murdered him.

1035
01:37:21,050 --> 01:37:22,190
What did you do to him?

1036
01:37:22,470 --> 01:37:23,310
He sent arrestees.

1037
01:37:23,630 --> 01:37:24,310
He killed them both.

1038
01:37:25,070 --> 01:37:26,450
But he killed his own mother?

1039
01:37:27,950 --> 01:37:29,010
He avenged his father.

1040
01:37:30,050 --> 01:37:31,030
You don't have to worry.

1041
01:37:31,630 --> 01:37:33,810
And your mother has nothing in common with
Clytemnestra.

1042
01:37:34,510 --> 01:37:35,150
Maybe not.

1043
01:37:35,850 --> 01:37:38,410
But the suitors have convinced the elders
it's time she remarried.

1044
01:37:39,270 --> 01:37:41,190
It's not a good time for an empty throne.

1045
01:37:41,970 --> 01:37:43,970
He heard the stories, people from the sea.

1046
01:37:44,730 --> 01:37:45,350
Have you been raided?

1047
01:37:45,630 --> 01:37:46,810
Not yet, but they did tax.

1048
01:37:46,970 --> 01:37:47,930
They do seem closer.

1049
01:37:48,470 --> 01:37:49,230
The spotter's ready.

1050
01:37:49,770 --> 01:37:50,350
What about Ithaca?

1051
01:37:51,210 --> 01:37:53,350
She'll be able to raise an army,
defend herself.

1052
01:37:53,350 --> 01:37:54,910
Someone's got to take the throne.

1053
01:37:55,190 --> 01:37:57,150
Then the battle is there, not here,
son.

1054
01:37:58,230 --> 01:37:59,530
You've run from the problem.

1055
01:38:00,630 --> 01:38:03,030
I left your mother the mercy of the
suitors.

1056
01:38:04,390 --> 01:38:05,790
Should I hire mercenaries?

1057
01:38:06,650 --> 01:38:07,650
Go back and kill them all?

1058
01:38:08,690 --> 01:38:11,150
Murder rich and powerful guests in your
own home.

1059
01:38:11,810 --> 01:38:12,590
You'd be exiled.

1060
01:38:13,530 --> 01:38:14,250
What then?

1061
01:38:14,930 --> 01:38:17,430
You must evict them without violating
Zeus' law.

1062
01:38:19,650 --> 01:38:21,230
I have to take the throne.

1063
01:38:22,350 --> 01:38:24,170
I have to know if Odysseus is alive.

1064
01:38:24,910 --> 01:38:27,550
This is nothing to do with the father
you've never known.

1065
01:38:28,310 --> 01:38:29,690
You have to lead, son.

1066
01:38:30,630 --> 01:38:31,910
No matter what's happened to Odysseus.

1067
01:38:45,950 --> 01:38:47,230
You need to remember.

1068
01:38:47,850 --> 01:38:48,510
No, I don't.

1069
01:38:49,510 --> 01:38:50,950
I don't need to know the truth.

1070
01:38:51,130 --> 01:38:52,390
I'm happy here with you.

1071
01:38:54,610 --> 01:38:58,730
And why are you gathering the bones of
your old ship to make a raft?

1072
01:39:01,250 --> 01:39:02,430
Remember your crew.

1073
01:39:04,150 --> 01:39:05,030
Your journey.

1074
01:39:09,450 --> 01:39:11,310
I heard the song of the sirens.

1075
01:39:21,180 --> 01:39:22,780
Put the wax in the men's ears.

1076
01:39:23,980 --> 01:39:24,700
Not for me.

1077
01:39:25,080 --> 01:39:25,760
Tie me to the mast.

1078
01:39:26,300 --> 01:39:26,600
Why?

1079
01:39:27,160 --> 01:39:28,500
I want to hear the sirens' song.

1080
01:39:29,060 --> 01:39:29,260
No.

1081
01:39:30,180 --> 01:39:31,180
It's a bad idea.

1082
01:39:31,980 --> 01:39:33,180
No one's ever heard the song and lived.

1083
01:39:33,460 --> 01:39:34,480
That's why it's a good idea.

1084
01:39:34,720 --> 01:39:35,340
I'll be the first.

1085
01:39:36,500 --> 01:39:40,560
No matter how hard I plead, do not untie
me until we're well clear.

1086
01:39:41,740 --> 01:39:42,840
Check the men's ears.

1087
01:39:44,040 --> 01:39:45,740
A week's rations for the first.

1088
01:39:45,940 --> 01:39:46,480
Yes, sir!

1089
01:40:44,760 --> 01:40:46,580
What was the siren's song?

1090
01:40:48,200 --> 01:40:49,660
All the things he wanted to be.

1091
01:40:51,720 --> 01:40:54,300
Then, all the things he wished he'd never
wished for.

1092
01:41:01,960 --> 01:41:02,620
Ah!

1093
01:41:11,840 --> 01:41:15,200
It was the delicious itch you go to
scratch.

1094
01:41:16,500 --> 01:41:19,360
The find that's under the skin,
you can't reach it.

1095
01:41:21,580 --> 01:41:24,220
So the delicious itch becomes unbearable.

1096
01:41:27,900 --> 01:41:32,160
I told you what you most want...

1097
01:41:32,160 --> 01:41:34,560
is what you most can't have.

1098
01:41:37,660 --> 01:41:40,860
And what you most can't have...

1099
01:41:40,860 --> 01:41:44,140
is what you already had...

1100
01:41:44,140 --> 01:41:44,800
and lost.

1101
01:41:53,330 --> 01:41:56,350
It was the song of all the promises I
failed to keep.

1102
01:42:01,160 --> 01:42:03,560
And it told me I don't really want to go
home.

1103
01:42:08,670 --> 01:42:10,130
My water ahead!

1104
01:42:12,930 --> 01:42:13,550
Corribed us.

1105
01:42:15,290 --> 01:42:16,730
A monstrous whirlpool.

1106
01:42:19,670 --> 01:42:20,550
Hoist the sails!

1107
01:42:21,190 --> 01:42:21,770
Come on, let's go!

1108
01:42:22,590 --> 01:42:23,790
It's a wizard's job!

1109
01:42:24,650 --> 01:42:25,490
Can we head out to sea?

1110
01:42:25,810 --> 01:42:26,710
It's already got us!

1111
01:42:29,070 --> 01:42:29,950
And don't go!

1112
01:42:30,650 --> 01:42:32,110
We want to have a roost movement!

1113
01:42:33,650 --> 01:42:34,910
We might make that canyon.

1114
01:42:35,110 --> 01:42:35,810
It's too narrow.

1115
01:42:36,470 --> 01:42:37,570
Make for the whirlpool.

1116
01:42:38,090 --> 01:42:41,850
If you steer down the slope, we can make
enough way to shoot out the other side.

1117
01:42:42,010 --> 01:42:42,890
Don't be afraid.

1118
01:42:43,030 --> 01:42:43,610
We can do it.

1119
01:42:48,770 --> 01:42:49,310
No.

1120
01:42:49,930 --> 01:42:50,210
No!

1121
01:42:51,150 --> 01:42:52,050
We're making for the channel.

1122
01:42:52,810 --> 01:42:54,130
We're making for the channel!

1123
01:42:56,810 --> 01:42:57,350
Prepare

1124
01:43:00,540 --> 01:43:01,760
to hold water!

1125
01:43:09,020 --> 01:43:10,000
On my signal!

1126
01:44:08,500 --> 01:44:09,580
We did it!

1127
01:44:10,080 --> 01:44:11,040
We did it!

1128
01:44:11,040 --> 01:44:12,600
You guys...

1129
01:44:23,120 --> 01:44:24,400
Keep us straight!

1130
01:45:00,380 --> 01:45:00,900
You knew!

1131
01:45:02,060 --> 01:45:03,080
And you didn't tell us!

1132
01:45:03,640 --> 01:45:06,020
If I told you, you wouldn't have chosen
this way.

1133
01:45:06,400 --> 01:45:08,560
And those men would still be alive!

1134
01:45:08,900 --> 01:45:08,960
No!

1135
01:45:09,320 --> 01:45:10,140
We'd all be dead!

1136
01:45:10,800 --> 01:45:11,740
You had no right!

1137
01:45:12,100 --> 01:45:13,040
I had every right!

1138
01:45:13,540 --> 01:45:15,140
And every responsibility!

1139
01:45:15,680 --> 01:45:18,260
The whirlpool would have destroyed the
ship and all of us!

1140
01:45:18,640 --> 01:45:19,640
This way we live!

1141
01:45:19,900 --> 01:45:20,920
Some of us live!

1142
01:45:20,920 --> 01:45:21,620
Yes!

1143
01:45:25,340 --> 01:45:27,240
What else did Tyrese just tell you?

1144
01:45:38,860 --> 01:45:40,160
I'm going to put in there.

1145
01:45:42,760 --> 01:45:44,400
We can't land there.

1146
01:45:44,660 --> 01:45:46,060
That's the sun god's isle.

1147
01:45:46,440 --> 01:45:47,120
We need repairs!

1148
01:45:48,880 --> 01:45:50,380
These men are exhausted!

1149
01:45:50,860 --> 01:45:52,080
I lost six shipmates!

1150
01:45:52,780 --> 01:45:55,300
You're going to put in and rest up on dry
land.

1151
01:45:55,660 --> 01:45:56,760
You're all going to die!

1152
01:45:59,600 --> 01:46:01,900
You asked me what Tyrese has told me.

1153
01:46:02,900 --> 01:46:04,240
He said you're all going to die.

1154
01:46:05,060 --> 01:46:10,880
If you slaughter the sun god's cattle,
which graze on that island, you'll all

1155
01:46:10,880 --> 01:46:11,200
die.

1156
01:46:15,580 --> 01:46:16,380
That's fine.

1157
01:46:17,660 --> 01:46:18,400
We'll put in.

1158
01:46:19,520 --> 01:46:20,740
We'll cook our own provisions.

1159
01:46:21,240 --> 01:46:21,940
We'll pick fruit.

1160
01:46:22,080 --> 01:46:22,660
We'll get water.

1161
01:46:23,040 --> 01:46:23,760
Nothing more.

1162
01:46:25,420 --> 01:46:26,220
Swear to it.

1163
01:46:26,940 --> 01:46:27,840
All of you.

1164
01:46:42,050 --> 01:46:42,570
See?

1165
01:46:44,050 --> 01:46:45,450
The wind took us right in.

1166
01:46:46,810 --> 01:46:47,610
We'll rest up.

1167
01:46:48,710 --> 01:46:49,370
Do the repairs.

1168
01:46:49,890 --> 01:46:51,370
We'll set out as soon as the wind changes.

1169
01:46:52,670 --> 01:46:53,530
Grab that line.

1170
01:47:04,760 --> 01:47:06,440
Did the crew keep their promise?

1171
01:47:07,880 --> 01:47:08,320
Yes.

1172
01:47:09,520 --> 01:47:10,720
For a long time.

1173
01:47:12,740 --> 01:47:14,180
But the wind never changed.

1174
01:47:14,660 --> 01:47:18,080
The steady onshore breeze that made it so
easy for us to land.

1175
01:47:20,660 --> 01:47:22,400
Made it impossible to leave.

1176
01:47:26,630 --> 01:47:28,230
And our provisions ran out.

1177
01:48:34,560 --> 01:48:38,020
Did you think if you ate quietly,
the sun god wouldn't notice?

1178
01:48:38,920 --> 01:48:39,960
We thought you wouldn't notice.

1179
01:48:41,040 --> 01:48:42,120
I'm not the problem.

1180
01:48:42,660 --> 01:48:43,560
I wanted you to live.

1181
01:48:49,600 --> 01:48:50,740
Tyrese has told you.

1182
01:48:51,240 --> 01:48:52,080
Cersei told you.

1183
01:48:52,200 --> 01:48:53,040
The gods told you.

1184
01:48:54,000 --> 01:48:56,240
You gods learn speak in ways we
understand.

1185
01:48:56,660 --> 01:48:57,940
Who doesn't understand pain?

1186
01:48:59,020 --> 01:49:00,240
Or blood or death?

1187
01:49:01,340 --> 01:49:04,080
When you watch your men die, do you see
chance or do you see consequence?

1188
01:49:05,200 --> 01:49:06,100
I see consequence.

1189
01:49:06,600 --> 01:49:07,360
I see my failure.

1190
01:49:08,240 --> 01:49:10,140
What if their deaths aren't your failure
for theirs?

1191
01:49:11,020 --> 01:49:11,780
I lead them.

1192
01:49:13,500 --> 01:49:14,560
I led them here.

1193
01:49:15,400 --> 01:49:16,140
For no led you.

1194
01:49:17,700 --> 01:49:19,440
Why do you put yourself above the gods?

1195
01:49:19,560 --> 01:49:20,960
Why do you claim responsibility?

1196
01:49:22,120 --> 01:49:23,600
Why don't you want to go home?

1197
01:49:24,100 --> 01:49:25,020
What do you expect?

1198
01:49:26,940 --> 01:49:28,360
They ran out of food.

1199
01:49:29,620 --> 01:49:30,600
Have some pity.

1200
01:49:30,920 --> 01:49:33,620
My pity is what put us on this damn
island.

1201
01:49:33,920 --> 01:49:35,040
I told you what would happen.

1202
01:49:35,380 --> 01:49:35,940
Yes, you did.

1203
01:49:36,780 --> 01:49:39,640
And we decided we would rather drown than
starve.

1204
01:49:45,480 --> 01:49:47,500
Well, now's our chance.

1205
01:49:48,060 --> 01:49:49,020
The wind has dropped!

1206
01:49:49,380 --> 01:49:50,340
The wind has dropped!

1207
01:49:53,860 --> 01:49:57,680
The calm was just long enough to lure us
out on the Poseidon's ocean.

1208
01:49:59,040 --> 01:50:00,280
Under Zeus's sky.

1209
01:51:52,090 --> 01:51:55,350
The last thing I remember was the harsh
hand of Zeus.

1210
01:51:56,890 --> 01:51:57,170
I

1211
01:52:40,780 --> 01:52:41,960
don't know what happened to my men.

1212
01:52:42,320 --> 01:52:42,940
Yes, you do.

1213
01:52:45,180 --> 01:52:46,900
Exactly what you told them would happen.

1214
01:52:47,780 --> 01:52:49,460
I washed up on your shore alone.

1215
01:52:49,920 --> 01:52:51,000
You washed up on your shore alone?

1216
01:52:51,000 --> 01:52:54,280
I washed up on my shore with some of your
boat and none of your crew.

1217
01:52:56,540 --> 01:52:58,280
Half dead, unconscious.

1218
01:53:21,240 --> 01:53:26,840
I watched you lost for days and two weeks.

1219
01:53:27,860 --> 01:53:34,000
I knew the lotus would ease your awakening
so that your pain was more of the body

1220
01:53:34,000 --> 01:53:34,700
than the mind.

1221
01:53:37,460 --> 01:53:38,380
And it did.

1222
01:53:41,140 --> 01:53:42,760
You liked it so much.

1223
01:53:44,280 --> 01:53:45,880
You ate it so willingly.

1224
01:53:49,720 --> 01:53:51,360
And as I cared for you...

1225
01:53:52,160 --> 01:53:52,580
Thank you.

1226
01:53:53,280 --> 01:53:54,400
I came to love you.

1227
01:54:00,020 --> 01:54:01,480
How long have I been here?

1228
01:54:03,820 --> 01:54:08,100
I kept telling myself to stop giving it to
you that you were strong enough to

1229
01:54:08,100 --> 01:54:08,480
remember.

1230
01:54:08,480 --> 01:54:09,840
How long collapsed on me?

1231
01:54:09,840 --> 01:54:10,920
You were so happy.

1232
01:54:11,180 --> 01:54:11,860
You were happy.

1233
01:54:12,000 --> 01:54:14,420
We were so happy for years.

1234
01:54:15,180 --> 01:54:15,460
Years?

1235
01:54:22,530 --> 01:54:23,550
Seven years.

1236
01:54:24,510 --> 01:54:28,250
You fed me lotus flower and held me
captive.

1237
01:54:28,630 --> 01:54:29,810
I saved your life.

1238
01:54:30,930 --> 01:54:32,150
I rebuilt your body.

1239
01:54:32,290 --> 01:54:34,870
I just couldn't bring myself to rebuild
your mind.

1240
01:54:36,170 --> 01:54:37,490
You have been happy.

1241
01:54:37,490 --> 01:54:41,650
Your heart was just always somewhere else.

1242
01:54:42,910 --> 01:54:46,050
And your mind was bound to follow
eventually.

1243
01:54:47,550 --> 01:54:48,350
How could you?

1244
01:54:48,990 --> 01:54:54,110
The lotus clouds the mind and saps the
well, but it doesn't change who you are.

1245
01:54:54,950 --> 01:54:57,630
You weren't ready to go home for whatever
reason.

1246
01:54:58,270 --> 01:54:59,070
I am now.

1247
01:55:00,610 --> 01:55:02,410
As I always knew you would be.

1248
01:55:03,490 --> 01:55:03,810
Now?

1249
01:55:04,330 --> 01:55:06,950
You were clutching something when I found
you.

1250
01:55:06,950 --> 01:55:08,890
Your hand wouldn't open for weeks.

1251
01:55:09,630 --> 01:55:10,890
You'd call out.

1252
01:55:11,710 --> 01:55:15,930
Mostly to Athena, but sometimes you would
say another name.

1253
01:55:21,230 --> 01:55:21,910
You're

1254
01:55:40,460 --> 01:55:42,200
a man who needs to control his fate.

1255
01:55:43,760 --> 01:55:45,340
But you cannot control this.

1256
01:55:48,520 --> 01:55:50,700
You must give yourself to the storm.

1257
01:55:51,460 --> 01:55:53,260
Give yourself to Poseidon.

1258
01:55:54,520 --> 01:55:55,940
And take your punishment.

1259
01:55:58,880 --> 01:56:00,500
Just won't let him kill you.

1260
01:56:04,130 --> 01:56:05,430
Give up the fight.

1261
01:56:05,690 --> 01:56:07,350
Give up control and live.

1262
01:56:10,270 --> 01:56:13,590
And to leave a face you've never yet made.

1263
01:56:13,790 --> 01:56:14,330
Odysseus.

1264
01:57:06,640 --> 01:57:07,280
Odysseus.

1265
01:57:15,540 --> 01:57:16,540
Where am I?

1266
01:57:18,840 --> 01:57:20,460
Don't you recognize your own home?

1267
01:57:59,890 --> 01:58:01,350
Just welcomes you, strangers.

1268
01:58:02,390 --> 01:58:03,790
How many are you?

1269
01:58:04,590 --> 01:58:05,290
Half a dozen.

1270
01:58:06,250 --> 01:58:06,910
Need a meal?

1271
01:58:07,650 --> 01:58:08,270
Hand supplies.

1272
01:58:09,410 --> 01:58:09,970
For the road.

1273
01:58:10,810 --> 01:58:11,570
Where are you headed?

1274
01:58:12,090 --> 01:58:12,870
We need pigs.

1275
01:58:13,550 --> 01:58:14,970
We're going all the way to Pylos.

1276
01:58:15,690 --> 01:58:16,490
Are you pilgrims?

1277
01:58:17,690 --> 01:58:19,090
Do we look like pilgrims?

1278
01:58:19,550 --> 01:58:20,130
I don't see them.

1279
01:58:20,130 --> 01:58:20,490
Very well.

1280
01:58:21,710 --> 01:58:25,450
It's just a man's hour for you,
Emil, but these pigs aren't mine to sell.

1281
01:58:25,830 --> 01:58:26,450
Oh, don't worry.

1282
01:58:27,270 --> 01:58:28,150
We're not paying.

1283
01:58:31,770 --> 01:58:33,770
Give my greetings to your mother.

1284
01:58:37,160 --> 01:58:38,340
Tell her, I'm sorry.

1285
01:58:38,940 --> 01:58:41,660
I'm sorry for Troy and for everything that
has happened.

1286
01:58:49,300 --> 01:58:51,060
I'm sorry I had no news of your father.

1287
01:58:51,500 --> 01:58:52,480
No, you had news.

1288
01:58:53,140 --> 01:58:54,880
Not of where he is, but who he is.

1289
01:58:56,260 --> 01:58:58,680
And no more than ever, I hope,
to one day meet him.

1290
01:59:03,900 --> 01:59:05,860
Where are your watchdogs, old man?

1291
01:59:10,580 --> 01:59:12,040
I'm burning their bodies.

1292
01:59:17,170 --> 01:59:19,330
Can Zeus offer you food, Drake,
stranger?

1293
01:59:20,690 --> 01:59:21,390
Were you attacked?

1294
01:59:22,570 --> 01:59:23,910
The dog saved your farmhands.

1295
01:59:24,610 --> 01:59:26,350
We burned his brothers first.

1296
01:59:29,450 --> 01:59:30,770
What happened to Ithaca?

1297
01:59:31,710 --> 01:59:32,350
Darkness.

1298
01:59:33,210 --> 01:59:35,230
Zeus' laws smashed to pieces.

1299
01:59:36,350 --> 01:59:39,410
A kingdom without a king since my master
died.

1300
01:59:39,950 --> 01:59:40,430
Your master?

1301
01:59:41,970 --> 01:59:42,610
Odysseus.

1302
01:59:43,490 --> 01:59:44,990
Hero of the Trojan War.

1303
01:59:47,270 --> 01:59:48,830
But Odysseus is alive.

1304
01:59:49,470 --> 01:59:51,130
Because I'm a father, I hope!

1305
01:59:51,610 --> 01:59:53,170
No more, no more!

1306
01:59:53,630 --> 01:59:57,090
No more, no more, no more, no more,
no more, no!

1307
01:59:57,090 --> 01:59:58,350
Dilemicus.

1308
01:59:58,890 --> 02:00:00,390
Dilemicus, Dilemicus.

1309
02:00:00,870 --> 02:00:01,450
Dilemicus.

1310
02:00:02,790 --> 02:00:04,990
He's in danger, and I can't help him.

1311
02:00:05,530 --> 02:00:06,310
There must be others.

1312
02:00:07,810 --> 02:00:08,890
Odysseus' son.

1313
02:00:09,130 --> 02:00:11,030
The powers is controlled by suitors.

1314
02:00:12,070 --> 02:00:14,250
No one is still loyal to Odysseus.

1315
02:00:15,610 --> 02:00:16,530
What about the queen?

1316
02:00:17,570 --> 02:00:19,690
Beautiful wife Penelope.

1317
02:00:20,430 --> 02:00:22,890
Held hostage by the worst men.

1318
02:00:23,230 --> 02:00:25,290
Soon to be forced into marriage.

1319
02:00:25,290 --> 02:00:27,510
What can she do without even her son?

1320
02:00:28,050 --> 02:00:28,770
Yeah, drink.

1321
02:00:33,930 --> 02:00:35,590
What's the danger to Dilemicus?

1322
02:00:36,630 --> 02:00:38,690
He's on his way back from Spartan.

1323
02:00:40,070 --> 02:00:44,350
The suitors send bandits to Pylos to
ambush him at the temple.

1324
02:00:45,310 --> 02:00:46,590
Give me food for the road.

1325
02:00:47,530 --> 02:00:50,030
You'll never catch up with the bandits.

1326
02:00:50,610 --> 02:00:53,650
If I can get to Pylos before Dilemicus
arrives, I can warn him.

1327
02:00:54,350 --> 02:00:55,370
Why are you helping?

1328
02:00:56,130 --> 02:00:58,210
If I tell you, don't be angry.

1329
02:00:58,970 --> 02:01:00,790
I served with Odysseus at Troy.

1330
02:01:01,150 --> 02:01:01,510
Really?

1331
02:01:02,830 --> 02:01:04,670
He was a harsh taskmaster.

1332
02:01:04,930 --> 02:01:05,750
Oh, but fair.

1333
02:01:07,310 --> 02:01:09,350
Fair and worthy of following.

1334
02:01:10,870 --> 02:01:11,750
You loved him?

1335
02:01:12,870 --> 02:01:13,810
Like a son.

1336
02:01:14,770 --> 02:01:17,090
Well, he's not dead.

1337
02:01:17,910 --> 02:01:19,350
She's coming home.

1338
02:01:20,350 --> 02:01:20,990
Soon?

1339
02:01:21,890 --> 02:01:23,110
Bringing vengeance.

1340
02:01:25,710 --> 02:01:26,850
Bringing it all.

1341
02:01:36,430 --> 02:01:37,090
I'll talk.

1342
02:01:37,490 --> 02:01:39,190
You don't want people realizing where you
are.

1343
02:01:39,550 --> 02:01:41,050
Well, maybe we shouldn't have brought a
guard.

1344
02:01:41,730 --> 02:01:43,750
We'll be glad we did if we have trouble on
the road.

1345
02:02:00,450 --> 02:02:01,670
You may have sent me.

1346
02:02:04,010 --> 02:02:04,930
Find that beggar.

1347
02:02:21,230 --> 02:02:22,490
Your men must wait out here.

1348
02:02:23,050 --> 02:02:23,610
The weapons, too.

1349
02:02:40,880 --> 02:02:42,280
His use welcomes you, strangers.

1350
02:02:42,840 --> 02:02:44,120
Have you traveled far?

1351
02:02:44,980 --> 02:02:45,700
Not that far.

1352
02:02:47,080 --> 02:02:48,880
You're here to rest for the night.

1353
02:02:51,020 --> 02:02:53,080
We're here to make sacrifice to Athena.

1354
02:02:55,320 --> 02:02:58,280
Then whatever hospitality you can offer
might be considered.

1355
02:02:58,840 --> 02:02:59,320
Of course.

1356
02:02:59,880 --> 02:03:01,080
Get that beggar out of here.

1357
02:03:01,180 --> 02:03:02,380
He has a message for me.

1358
02:03:03,420 --> 02:03:04,620
You may have sent you.

1359
02:03:05,980 --> 02:03:06,660
Yes, sir.

1360
02:03:12,760 --> 02:03:13,980
But what's the message?

1361
02:03:15,500 --> 02:03:16,420
He's private.

1362
02:03:17,080 --> 02:03:20,780
These men are pr- These men are pr-

1363
02:03:37,970 --> 02:03:40,390
This is your moment to leave, old man.

1364
02:03:41,250 --> 02:03:42,130
You got to be a stranger.

1365
02:03:44,750 --> 02:03:46,930
I already spared one old man this week.

1366
02:03:47,510 --> 02:03:48,530
But he was blind.

1367
02:03:49,390 --> 02:03:50,590
I won't spare him.

1368
02:03:58,010 --> 02:03:59,290
Let's go.

1369
02:04:29,000 --> 02:04:30,700
It's not bad for a beggar.

1370
02:04:31,500 --> 02:04:32,800
No one's born a beggar.

1371
02:04:33,500 --> 02:04:34,940
We'll take this priest's robes.

1372
02:04:35,360 --> 02:04:36,380
Put him over your armor.

1373
02:04:38,700 --> 02:04:39,840
Help me with his body.

1374
02:04:40,220 --> 02:04:41,000
We have to leave him.

1375
02:04:41,700 --> 02:04:42,040
No.

1376
02:04:42,300 --> 02:04:43,280
We have to slip out.

1377
02:04:44,120 --> 02:04:45,540
That's not how you honor the dead.

1378
02:04:45,760 --> 02:04:46,960
That is how you stay alive.

1379
02:04:47,180 --> 02:04:48,040
He was my teacher.

1380
02:04:49,500 --> 02:04:50,900
He might have been Athena in disguise.

1381
02:04:51,200 --> 02:04:52,240
His name was Mentor.

1382
02:04:52,980 --> 02:04:55,780
And when you are safe, we'll travel to the
unknown west.

1383
02:04:56,560 --> 02:04:57,520
And we will honor him.

1384
02:04:58,100 --> 02:04:59,560
These men were sent by the suitors.

1385
02:05:00,080 --> 02:05:02,160
There will be more of them we need to get
on the road.

1386
02:05:02,160 --> 02:05:03,060
By night?

1387
02:05:03,440 --> 02:05:04,540
Safer than staying here.

1388
02:05:05,200 --> 02:05:07,220
Mentor is at the heart of Athena's oldest
temple.

1389
02:05:08,040 --> 02:05:10,000
For now, we'll honor him in flame.

1390
02:05:48,880 --> 02:05:50,700
So how does a beggar come to know Mentor?

1391
02:05:51,100 --> 02:05:51,840
I told you.

1392
02:05:52,320 --> 02:05:53,420
I wasn't always a beggar.

1393
02:05:54,080 --> 02:05:55,120
You have wise eyes.

1394
02:05:56,360 --> 02:05:56,940
Athena's eyes.

1395
02:05:56,940 --> 02:05:58,240
Don't look for gods and men.

1396
02:05:58,660 --> 02:05:59,880
You'll just be disappointed.

1397
02:06:00,440 --> 02:06:01,900
Well, then tell me who you are.

1398
02:06:03,640 --> 02:06:04,900
My name is Sinon.

1399
02:06:04,900 --> 02:06:08,480
I'm an old soldier who fought under your
father at Troy.

1400
02:06:09,760 --> 02:06:10,860
You knew my father?

1401
02:06:11,580 --> 02:06:14,440
I've been taking orders from him for
longer than I care to remember.

1402
02:06:15,600 --> 02:06:16,560
Wait, he's alive?

1403
02:06:17,800 --> 02:06:18,580
He's alive.

1404
02:06:20,380 --> 02:06:21,600
Well, where is he?

1405
02:06:22,140 --> 02:06:23,160
He's close by.

1406
02:06:23,760 --> 02:06:26,140
He sent me ahead to assess the situation.

1407
02:06:27,820 --> 02:06:29,280
Well, mother's not gonna believe.

1408
02:06:29,760 --> 02:06:30,840
No, you can't tell her.

1409
02:06:32,020 --> 02:06:32,820
What, nothing?

1410
02:06:34,300 --> 02:06:35,020
Tell her.

1411
02:06:36,520 --> 02:06:38,880
Tell her he decided it's time she
remarried.

1412
02:06:40,660 --> 02:06:42,320
He doesn't need to test my mother.

1413
02:06:42,460 --> 02:06:43,180
How can he doubt her?

1414
02:06:43,620 --> 02:06:44,200
He doesn't.

1415
02:06:45,000 --> 02:06:47,160
But she has every reason to have forgotten
him.

1416
02:06:49,300 --> 02:06:50,540
Are there any loyal servants?

1417
02:06:51,840 --> 02:06:52,340
Some.

1418
02:06:53,660 --> 02:06:55,520
When Odysseus shows himself lost.

1419
02:06:57,040 --> 02:06:59,100
We need to gather the suitors at the
palace.

1420
02:07:00,160 --> 02:07:01,380
That's never a problem.

1421
02:07:02,040 --> 02:07:03,580
You have gifts for Menelaus?

1422
02:07:04,140 --> 02:07:05,000
Yeah, on my ship.

1423
02:07:05,740 --> 02:07:06,180
Good.

1424
02:07:07,220 --> 02:07:08,280
Come back as a prince.

1425
02:07:08,960 --> 02:07:09,640
Call a banquet.

1426
02:07:10,720 --> 02:07:11,380
I'll be there.

1427
02:07:12,540 --> 02:07:13,860
What will you do with the banquet?

1428
02:07:14,880 --> 02:07:15,240
The bag.

1429
02:07:16,240 --> 02:07:18,200
The clearest view of a man is from below.

1430
02:07:20,320 --> 02:07:21,640
How many suitors are there?

1431
02:07:22,560 --> 02:07:23,240
It doesn't.

1432
02:07:24,220 --> 02:07:26,040
If all of these men are like you,
then...

1433
02:07:26,040 --> 02:07:27,060
He doesn't have any men.

1434
02:07:28,100 --> 02:07:29,040
But it's just you?

1435
02:07:30,300 --> 02:07:31,280
And you?

1436
02:07:34,000 --> 02:07:34,720
It's

1437
02:07:38,590 --> 02:07:38,750
back.

1438
02:07:39,150 --> 02:07:39,590
It's back.

1439
02:07:41,250 --> 02:07:42,170
Who's seeing this?

1440
02:07:42,610 --> 02:07:43,570
Everyone's seeing this.

1441
02:07:44,290 --> 02:07:45,090
It's Salaamicus!

1442
02:07:45,790 --> 02:07:46,490
My queen!

1443
02:07:47,130 --> 02:07:48,430
Sure, your place is up there.

1444
02:07:56,770 --> 02:07:57,470
It's true.

1445
02:08:00,490 --> 02:08:01,490
You're home.

1446
02:08:02,650 --> 02:08:03,450
I'm back.

1447
02:08:04,410 --> 02:08:05,750
I thought tonight we could celebrate.

1448
02:08:06,490 --> 02:08:09,670
If we have anything left to celebrate
with.

1449
02:08:11,230 --> 02:08:12,090
Don't worry.

1450
02:08:12,990 --> 02:08:15,710
My brave suitors will shower us with gifts
to honor your return.

1451
02:08:16,170 --> 02:08:17,610
So glad to see your safe return.

1452
02:08:28,530 --> 02:08:29,910
Glad to see you on your feet.

1453
02:08:30,690 --> 02:08:32,450
It's about time we took you back to the
palace.

1454
02:08:36,150 --> 02:08:37,470
What did you hear about your father?

1455
02:08:39,750 --> 02:08:40,250
Nothing.

1456
02:08:41,350 --> 02:08:44,970
A story is from the war, but no one knew
what happened to him after he left Troy.

1457
02:08:45,910 --> 02:08:46,530
I'm sorry, Mom.

1458
02:08:47,970 --> 02:08:51,210
Everyone in Sparta is talking about a
coming catastrophe.

1459
02:08:52,290 --> 02:08:56,270
An age of darkness brought on by people
from the sea violating Zeus' law.

1460
02:08:56,510 --> 02:08:57,490
Some stories are true.

1461
02:08:57,910 --> 02:08:59,430
Our civilization is collapsing.

1462
02:09:00,170 --> 02:09:02,790
Menelaus is concerned that we can't raise
an army with an empty throne.

1463
02:09:03,290 --> 02:09:04,550
So we came up with a plan.

1464
02:09:06,270 --> 02:09:07,030
What plan?

1465
02:09:09,470 --> 02:09:11,290
The one way to get the suitors to leave.

1466
02:09:13,850 --> 02:09:15,050
It's time for you to choose.

1467
02:09:19,230 --> 02:09:20,390
You want me to remarry?

1468
02:09:20,950 --> 02:09:23,570
If you remarry, the other suitors will
leave.

1469
02:09:23,970 --> 02:09:25,610
And then I'm only dealing with one of
them.

1470
02:09:26,090 --> 02:09:26,990
I'm almost of age.

1471
02:09:27,090 --> 02:09:29,850
I'll tell the elders that I learned of
Odysseus' death in Sparta.

1472
02:09:31,190 --> 02:09:33,970
Menelaus will back me up and they'll let
me assume the throne.

1473
02:09:34,150 --> 02:09:37,550
So you deny your father and I leave my
home and go live with one of these snakes.

1474
02:09:37,670 --> 02:09:38,190
That's your plan.

1475
02:09:38,190 --> 02:09:39,790
Would you have a better one?

1476
02:09:42,730 --> 02:09:45,790
Lock the door, set a fire and burn the
bastards.

1477
02:09:47,070 --> 02:09:50,730
Do you think if I remarry, life goes back
to the way it was?

1478
02:09:52,010 --> 02:09:53,510
That world is gone.

1479
02:09:54,030 --> 02:09:56,830
And in this one, they're not giving up
power to you.

1480
02:09:57,430 --> 02:09:58,370
Or Odysseus.

1481
02:09:58,570 --> 02:10:00,450
If he returned, he'd have to kill him.

1482
02:10:00,850 --> 02:10:01,750
Like you will.

1483
02:10:02,670 --> 02:10:05,890
If I kill them, their families will demand
revenge.

1484
02:10:06,010 --> 02:10:06,910
I'd be exiled.

1485
02:10:08,190 --> 02:10:10,010
And without me, the throne will be lost.

1486
02:10:10,110 --> 02:10:11,910
Because only a man can sit on it.

1487
02:10:13,710 --> 02:10:14,490
Empty throne?

1488
02:10:16,290 --> 02:10:19,090
I've been sitting on that empty throne for
20 years.

1489
02:10:20,010 --> 02:10:24,230
My knowledge, my years of experience,
that's all nothing compared for the

1490
02:10:24,230 --> 02:10:25,130
bristles on your chin.

1491
02:10:28,030 --> 02:10:29,070
Do you want peace?

1492
02:10:30,510 --> 02:10:31,470
Or do you want revenge?

1493
02:10:31,610 --> 02:10:32,310
I want a distance!

1494
02:10:32,310 --> 02:10:33,750
A face!

1495
02:10:35,790 --> 02:10:38,730
Sing, muse of a face!

1496
02:10:40,470 --> 02:10:43,070
A face!

1497
02:10:46,290 --> 02:10:48,690
A fleet!

1498
02:10:58,920 --> 02:10:59,840
Atrore!

1499
02:11:03,960 --> 02:11:05,720
Sing, muse of a man!

1500
02:11:08,700 --> 02:11:10,880
A man!

1501
02:11:24,830 --> 02:11:26,690
You may as go ahead.

1502
02:11:33,670 --> 02:11:34,550
Vargas.

1503
02:11:57,100 --> 02:11:59,280
You should bury him up on the cliffs.

1504
02:12:01,340 --> 02:12:02,480
Where you met?

1505
02:12:11,410 --> 02:12:12,670
You're Odysseus.

1506
02:12:15,570 --> 02:12:17,070
You're my father.

1507
02:12:19,690 --> 02:12:21,050
I'm a beggar.

1508
02:12:24,710 --> 02:12:27,030
And you...

1509
02:12:27,670 --> 02:12:33,470
are the worthy, beautiful son of Odysseus.

1510
02:12:34,550 --> 02:12:41,810
Of whom, when you do finally meet,
he will be so proud.

1511
02:12:46,040 --> 02:12:48,300
Welcome home, stranger.

1512
02:12:52,020 --> 02:12:56,660
Because the darkness of victory shrouds
the writers' legends.

1513
02:12:58,980 --> 02:13:01,540
And make boards to blind beggars of us
all.

1514
02:13:09,250 --> 02:13:11,030
Accept the hospitality of Zeus.

1515
02:13:11,790 --> 02:13:13,150
Get the strangers some food.

1516
02:13:13,830 --> 02:13:14,250
No, sir.

1517
02:13:15,370 --> 02:13:17,190
I'll just make the rounds with a bowl.

1518
02:13:22,820 --> 02:13:25,440
Had to invite another one of these filthy
platelikers to dinner.

1519
02:13:25,440 --> 02:13:27,420
What's one more beggar at this feast?

1520
02:13:45,640 --> 02:13:48,120
We need to kill him before he regains his
position with the elders.

1521
02:13:48,720 --> 02:13:48,920
Ow.

1522
02:14:07,570 --> 02:14:09,330
Get him to grab one of the weapons.

1523
02:14:14,050 --> 02:14:16,450
He's not the same young pup we used to be.

1524
02:14:17,130 --> 02:14:19,370
When you come back tonight, let me cut his
top.

1525
02:14:20,750 --> 02:14:21,770
Fuck off, old woman.

1526
02:14:22,410 --> 02:14:22,690
Please.

1527
02:14:23,310 --> 02:14:24,670
It's been days since I've eaten.

1528
02:14:24,690 --> 02:14:25,930
I told you to fuck off.

1529
02:14:25,930 --> 02:14:28,710
We can't be generous with food that's not
even yours.

1530
02:14:30,870 --> 02:14:32,270
There's no room in your bowl.

1531
02:14:32,750 --> 02:14:33,430
Yes, there is.

1532
02:14:39,360 --> 02:14:40,280
So, there is.

1533
02:14:42,140 --> 02:14:42,900
There you go.

1534
02:14:47,000 --> 02:14:49,260
I suppose you want something from me now.

1535
02:14:49,520 --> 02:14:49,840
No.

1536
02:14:50,860 --> 02:14:52,620
I have something for you.

1537
02:14:53,640 --> 02:14:54,780
You have something from me?

1538
02:14:55,800 --> 02:14:57,420
You have something from me!

1539
02:14:59,160 --> 02:15:05,400
What could a filthy old plateliker like
you possibly have that I could want?

1540
02:15:06,180 --> 02:15:08,180
Actually, it's yours.

1541
02:15:13,720 --> 02:15:15,560
I was asked to return it.

1542
02:15:17,660 --> 02:15:18,780
It's not mine.

1543
02:15:20,640 --> 02:15:21,320
No?

1544
02:15:25,020 --> 02:15:26,260
Where did you get this?

1545
02:15:28,800 --> 02:15:30,740
Don't turn your back on me!

1546
02:15:36,000 --> 02:15:36,720
It's Ineos.

1547
02:15:37,480 --> 02:15:39,260
You can't treat a beggar like that.

1548
02:15:39,360 --> 02:15:40,140
It's Zeus's law.

1549
02:15:41,020 --> 02:15:41,860
Zeus's law.

1550
02:15:46,060 --> 02:15:47,700
You think this is a guard?

1551
02:15:50,920 --> 02:15:52,880
You think this is a guard in disguise?

1552
02:15:54,360 --> 02:15:54,860
Are you?

1553
02:15:57,070 --> 02:15:57,960
Are you a guard?

1554
02:15:59,680 --> 02:16:00,200
Plateliker?

1555
02:16:01,440 --> 02:16:02,140
No.

1556
02:16:03,100 --> 02:16:05,220
I'm a veteran of the Trojan War.

1557
02:16:06,060 --> 02:16:07,940
Veteran of the Trojan War?

1558
02:16:10,020 --> 02:16:12,100
And what's your name, veteran?

1559
02:16:16,660 --> 02:16:17,360
Sinai.

1560
02:16:20,920 --> 02:16:22,780
Does that name mean something to you?

1561
02:16:24,240 --> 02:16:24,580
No.

1562
02:16:26,200 --> 02:16:30,560
I use it to honor the bravest young man I
ever met.

1563
02:16:30,920 --> 02:16:31,420
Sinai?

1564
02:16:33,260 --> 02:16:34,800
Well, not just Shepard's boy.

1565
02:16:37,480 --> 02:16:38,840
I don't remember that.

1566
02:16:38,960 --> 02:16:41,180
Yeah, you wanted you to take his place.

1567
02:16:42,080 --> 02:16:42,960
Where did you find this?

1568
02:16:43,820 --> 02:16:44,880
Where he'll soon be.

1569
02:16:46,180 --> 02:16:46,980
Where is that?

1570
02:16:48,200 --> 02:16:48,920
Here it is.

1571
02:16:49,940 --> 02:16:50,620
Enough!

1572
02:16:55,140 --> 02:16:56,840
The feast is done!

1573
02:17:01,090 --> 02:17:01,930
Let's go.

1574
02:17:03,450 --> 02:17:04,710
It's not mine.

1575
02:17:13,510 --> 02:17:15,510
A queen would like to speak with you.

1576
02:17:15,950 --> 02:17:18,150
Stay until everyone's left in the fires of
love.

1577
02:18:08,960 --> 02:18:10,060
Hello, traveler.

1578
02:18:11,540 --> 02:18:12,140
My queen?

1579
02:18:13,280 --> 02:18:16,440
Melanthal, have your clay awash the
stranger's feet.

1580
02:18:17,000 --> 02:18:18,060
That's not necessary.

1581
02:18:18,340 --> 02:18:21,720
My husband insisted we honor guests as
family.

1582
02:18:22,620 --> 02:18:24,320
You know of the disease, my husband?

1583
02:18:25,160 --> 02:18:25,680
Of course.

1584
02:18:26,720 --> 02:18:27,300
Who are the songs?

1585
02:18:28,440 --> 02:18:29,740
I was at Troy, ma'am.

1586
02:18:30,620 --> 02:18:32,380
The songs probably seem silly.

1587
02:18:33,880 --> 02:18:35,140
They make me cry.

1588
02:18:36,460 --> 02:18:37,060
Why?

1589
02:18:38,120 --> 02:18:39,360
For what was lost.

1590
02:18:40,360 --> 02:18:41,220
For lives?

1591
02:18:42,500 --> 02:18:43,200
And the years.

1592
02:18:44,680 --> 02:18:45,440
Everything.

1593
02:18:53,240 --> 02:18:55,120
Did you know of disease at Troy?

1594
02:18:56,120 --> 02:18:56,700
I did.

1595
02:18:58,040 --> 02:19:01,060
Was there some detail of how he dressed?

1596
02:19:01,280 --> 02:19:01,960
Something he wore?

1597
02:19:02,820 --> 02:19:04,320
He wore a strange pin.

1598
02:19:05,260 --> 02:19:05,680
Strange?

1599
02:19:06,880 --> 02:19:07,640
Beautiful.

1600
02:19:08,720 --> 02:19:09,680
But out of place.

1601
02:19:10,660 --> 02:19:11,500
Describe it.

1602
02:19:25,750 --> 02:19:27,130
I'll get more water.

1603
02:19:31,550 --> 02:19:34,370
It was a beautiful gold pin.

1604
02:19:35,490 --> 02:19:38,390
A figure of Athena encircled by vines.

1605
02:19:47,110 --> 02:19:48,570
Did he wear it off?

1606
02:19:50,050 --> 02:19:51,610
He never took it off.

1607
02:20:11,250 --> 02:20:14,530
How is it that you made it home when a
disease could not?

1608
02:20:15,910 --> 02:20:16,890
I'm not home.

1609
02:20:17,690 --> 02:20:18,270
Not yet.

1610
02:20:19,250 --> 02:20:20,150
Why not?

1611
02:20:20,870 --> 02:20:23,330
It's not always an easy thing,
a homecoming.

1612
02:20:24,310 --> 02:20:26,490
That's as true for Odysseus as for me.

1613
02:20:27,890 --> 02:20:35,470
The Odysseus I knew would have found or
fought his way back to me no matter what.

1614
02:20:36,510 --> 02:20:39,750
What if the Odysseus you knew lost his
way?

1615
02:20:44,220 --> 02:20:56,860
What if one night in a strange city he saw
things that made him think the home he

1616
02:20:56,860 --> 02:21:00,560
knew couldn't possibly be there anymore?

1617
02:21:04,650 --> 02:21:08,110
What if when he left the belly of the
horse

1618
02:21:12,330 --> 02:21:14,110
and opened the gates of Troy

1619
02:21:19,660 --> 02:21:25,660
he saw ten years of rage pour into that
city in one night?

1620
02:23:42,480 --> 02:23:49,920
A gift, an offering of peace that they
took into their home.

1621
02:23:59,780 --> 02:24:09,640
We violated all that's ever sacred between
people and turned a viper

1622
02:24:12,700 --> 02:24:14,320
into a hunt.

1623
02:24:30,200 --> 02:24:38,960
To burn the walls of Troy was to burn the
world entire including his home.

1624
02:24:45,500 --> 02:24:49,700
What if he knew that very night

1625
02:24:52,780 --> 02:24:57,220
as he walked through fires of anarchy and
pain

1626
02:25:46,300 --> 02:25:49,720
and in the days of sweaty celebrations to
follow?

1627
02:25:55,830 --> 02:25:59,510
What if he knew exactly what he'd done?

1628
02:26:18,100 --> 02:26:19,780
What all of you had done?

1629
02:26:20,300 --> 02:26:21,400
One man's idea.

1630
02:26:23,000 --> 02:26:24,560
One man's trick.

1631
02:26:26,500 --> 02:26:28,960
To break Zeus's law forever.

1632
02:26:30,160 --> 02:26:40,140
We lived in a world of palaces and trade
and language blind to its beauty until we

1633
02:26:40,140 --> 02:26:40,720
broke it.

1634
02:26:43,200 --> 02:26:45,400
You are the people from the sea?

1635
02:26:48,040 --> 02:26:49,040
Yes, my queen.

1636
02:26:50,980 --> 02:26:56,400
The breaking of Zeus's law is spreading
and I play our age of bronze is collapsing

1637
02:26:56,400 --> 02:27:01,420
and maybe he couldn't bear to see the
ruins of what he'd done anywhere,

1638
02:27:02,340 --> 02:27:03,940
at least of all his home.

1639
02:27:13,750 --> 02:27:19,490
But Odysseus is alive and he'll be here
when you need him most.

1640
02:27:24,430 --> 02:27:26,850
I've been living in his ruins long enough.

1641
02:27:32,490 --> 02:27:33,810
Bring the suitors at dawn.

1642
02:27:34,390 --> 02:27:35,610
Remove the arms from the hall.

1643
02:27:36,170 --> 02:27:40,310
In a few hours one suitor will be happy
and the rest angry vengeful men.

1644
02:27:40,310 --> 02:27:44,390
Ron's draws a man on so we'll have no
weapons at the trial and the doors will be

1645
02:27:44,390 --> 02:27:44,810
locked.

1646
02:27:45,530 --> 02:27:47,590
I'll marry whoever completes my challenge.

1647
02:27:54,380 --> 02:27:56,040
How will we take on the suitors?

1648
02:27:57,160 --> 02:27:57,780
Not we.

1649
02:27:59,060 --> 02:28:00,620
They'll have the loans on my hands.

1650
02:28:01,300 --> 02:28:02,640
Or you'd face exile.

1651
02:28:03,880 --> 02:28:04,560
Won't you?

1652
02:28:05,840 --> 02:28:07,820
Just make sure they can't get those
weapons.

1653
02:28:08,400 --> 02:28:09,320
I'll see to it.

1654
02:28:10,320 --> 02:28:12,220
Even an arm, there are so many of them.

1655
02:28:13,280 --> 02:28:14,360
And you won't have a weapon.

1656
02:28:16,140 --> 02:28:17,220
Oh, I'll have a weapon.

1657
02:28:19,120 --> 02:28:20,440
Your mother's seen to that.

1658
02:28:23,260 --> 02:28:24,960
Gather the servants you trust.

1659
02:28:25,400 --> 02:28:27,300
Lock the arms in the upper storage room.

1660
02:29:20,370 --> 02:29:22,290
Who seems interested in the outcome?

1661
02:29:22,290 --> 02:29:23,810
But what's the trial?

1662
02:29:48,140 --> 02:29:49,280
For years.

1663
02:29:50,640 --> 02:29:55,500
While my husband's been gone, you've
commandeered this palace for your

1664
02:29:55,500 --> 02:29:56,160
feasting.

1665
02:29:57,020 --> 02:29:59,800
Your excuse was you wanted to win my hand
in marriage.

1666
02:30:03,360 --> 02:30:13,480
As I listened to your party, your fight
corrupt my servants and disrespect my

1667
02:30:13,480 --> 02:30:18,020
home, I wanted nothing more than for
Odysseus to return and see all this for

1668
02:30:18,020 --> 02:30:18,440
himself.

1669
02:30:25,000 --> 02:30:30,140
But last night, I realized he's not here
now.

1670
02:30:33,140 --> 02:30:34,660
He never will be.

1671
02:30:37,860 --> 02:30:38,560
So...

1672
02:30:38,560 --> 02:30:45,320
I stand, ready to marry anyone here who
can string this hunting bow and shoot an

1673
02:30:45,320 --> 02:30:46,960
arrow through those 12 axes.

1674
02:30:49,200 --> 02:30:50,220
Step up.

1675
02:30:51,940 --> 02:30:53,500
And show me who you are.

1676
02:30:55,400 --> 02:30:55,940
You may as.

1677
02:31:07,840 --> 02:31:08,840
Who's first?

1678
02:31:09,660 --> 02:31:10,480
Give me the bow.

1679
02:31:30,920 --> 02:31:33,160
It can't be done by you.

1680
02:31:33,160 --> 02:31:37,320
If it's a boy like Odysseus do with ease,
then you do it.

1681
02:31:38,320 --> 02:31:39,220
I'll wait my turn.

1682
02:31:40,280 --> 02:31:42,600
Why don't we warm the bow and oil it?

1683
02:31:44,000 --> 02:31:45,940
This bow hasn't been strung in years.

1684
02:32:19,130 --> 02:32:20,170
Your turn.

1685
02:32:21,090 --> 02:32:21,790
There's no need.

1686
02:32:25,130 --> 02:32:26,490
My queen has made her point.

1687
02:32:27,490 --> 02:32:28,710
We can't replace Odysseus.

1688
02:32:29,330 --> 02:32:30,450
Some of us have always known that.

1689
02:32:30,890 --> 02:32:34,430
Some of us would have followed that great
man to Troy if he'd let us.

1690
02:32:37,390 --> 02:32:38,830
But he never came back.

1691
02:32:41,110 --> 02:32:42,350
So choose the best of us here.

1692
02:32:44,330 --> 02:32:46,390
All of us who failed your test.

1693
02:32:47,250 --> 02:32:47,910
I haven't.

1694
02:32:53,130 --> 02:32:55,030
Somebody get these beggars out of here.

1695
02:32:55,750 --> 02:32:58,530
The doors are locked until the trial is
over.

1696
02:32:58,970 --> 02:33:00,410
The trial is over.

1697
02:33:01,450 --> 02:33:02,450
I want to try.

1698
02:33:03,170 --> 02:33:03,990
Give it a rest.

1699
02:33:05,770 --> 02:33:07,790
He has a right to try.

1700
02:33:10,350 --> 02:33:11,150
He may as well.

1701
02:33:11,750 --> 02:33:13,690
You're making a mockery of your mother's
wishes.

1702
02:33:13,930 --> 02:33:16,210
My mother's wishes are for you all to
burn.

1703
02:33:19,410 --> 02:33:19,930
I'm sorry.

1704
02:33:34,710 --> 02:33:36,890
Look at the expert at work.

1705
02:33:37,330 --> 02:33:37,890
Wait a minute.

1706
02:33:39,710 --> 02:33:40,890
Come on!

1707
02:33:41,430 --> 02:33:42,930
Oh, old man, forget it.

1708
02:34:42,200 --> 02:34:42,940
Odysseus.

1709
02:34:49,790 --> 02:34:52,170
Surely your place is down there.

1710
02:34:55,590 --> 02:34:56,310
Stop!

1711
02:35:03,690 --> 02:35:06,690
Is it him?

1712
02:35:08,130 --> 02:35:08,750
It's him!

1713
02:35:34,210 --> 02:35:35,530
Where are the weapons?

1714
02:35:36,010 --> 02:35:37,270
They're at the store room.

1715
02:35:37,550 --> 02:35:37,890
Get them!

1716
02:35:42,770 --> 02:35:43,870
What are you waiting for?

1717
02:35:44,510 --> 02:35:45,530
I'm going to kill them all!

1718
02:35:49,190 --> 02:35:50,290
Look out!

1719
02:35:55,150 --> 02:35:56,590
I told you.

1720
02:35:56,970 --> 02:35:58,330
Too loud, cowherd.

1721
02:35:58,330 --> 02:35:58,510
No!

1722
02:38:18,310 --> 02:38:19,190
Get the shields!

1723
02:38:27,730 --> 02:38:28,230
You

1724
02:40:25,470 --> 02:40:26,810
thought you could kill us all?

1725
02:40:27,950 --> 02:40:29,130
As long as I kill you.

1726
02:40:30,590 --> 02:40:31,330
Finish this.

1727
02:40:34,990 --> 02:40:35,490
Dad!

1728
02:40:51,970 --> 02:40:52,530
Dad!

1729
02:41:05,570 --> 02:41:13,410
When you get to Hades, tell my men I will
head west and honor them.

1730
02:41:19,640 --> 02:41:21,880
And tell Senneth...

1731
02:41:22,640 --> 02:41:25,140
I gave you back your shame.

1732
02:41:31,420 --> 02:41:32,180
What

1733
02:41:44,680 --> 02:41:45,440
happened?

1734
02:41:47,540 --> 02:41:48,380
Did it beat them?

1735
02:41:49,920 --> 02:41:50,780
Did she dead?

1736
02:41:52,460 --> 02:41:55,360
We broke the fragile bonds between men.

1737
02:42:03,950 --> 02:42:10,970
And for centuries of darkness to follow,
the stories of Troy will only be sung.

1738
02:43:36,600 --> 02:43:37,320
Odysseus.

1739
02:43:38,240 --> 02:43:40,640
My love, you get that?

1740
02:43:42,500 --> 02:43:43,180
At last.

1741
02:43:44,100 --> 02:43:45,060
In the end.

1742
02:43:46,820 --> 02:43:48,680
To see my beloved if I could.

1743
02:43:49,560 --> 02:43:50,700
One last time.

1744
02:43:51,840 --> 02:43:52,320
No.

1745
02:43:53,740 --> 02:43:54,620
No, no, you can't.

1746
02:43:54,740 --> 02:43:55,240
You can't.

1747
02:43:55,340 --> 02:43:55,680
You can't.

1748
02:43:55,700 --> 02:43:55,960
Not now.

1749
02:43:56,160 --> 02:43:56,680
Not now, please.

1750
02:43:56,880 --> 02:44:01,320
You can't die.

1751
02:44:02,740 --> 02:44:03,620
I'm not deaf.

1752
02:44:06,560 --> 02:44:07,040
Exile.

1753
02:44:15,360 --> 02:44:16,860
Till I'm against who we became.

1754
02:44:28,790 --> 02:44:30,210
Take your fastest ship.

1755
02:44:31,430 --> 02:44:32,510
And greatest crew.

1756
02:44:33,330 --> 02:44:34,950
And head for the horizon.

1757
02:44:36,390 --> 02:44:38,070
Jason, he is kidding, son.

1758
02:44:40,770 --> 02:44:42,630
Why will the stories only be sung?

1759
02:44:45,690 --> 02:44:49,190
Because songs will be all they have to
remember those of us who could write.

1760
02:44:54,670 --> 02:44:56,090
Civilization will rise again.

1761
02:45:02,830 --> 02:45:04,990
The new dawn will break over the darkened
world.

1762
02:45:08,810 --> 02:45:11,230
And our mistakes will once again be
forgotten.

